\section{Transformation}\label{sec:3}

Let the age-specific life-table death counts be denoted by $d^s_{t, i}$ or $d^s_{t, x}$, where $t$ denotes a year, $s$ represents females or males, and $i$ or $x$ denotes an age. For each year $t$, the life-table death counts sum to a radix~$10^5$.

\subsection{Centered log-ratio transformation}\label{sec:3.1}

The CLR transformation presents a mapping from the simplex to a hyperplane in Euclidean space, where principal component analysis can be applied. The transformation consists of the following steps:
\begin{asparaenum}[1)]
\item Compute the geometric mean function, estimated by a simple average
\begin{equation*}
\alpha^s_{n, x} = \exp\Big(\frac{1}{n}\sum^n_{t=1}\ln d^s_{t,x}\Big).
\end{equation*}
We compute the centered data by dividing each death count by its geometric mean
\begin{equation*}
\zeta^s_{t,x} = \frac{d^s_{t,x}}{\alpha_{n,x}^s}.
\end{equation*}
\item By taking the natural logarithm, we obtain
\begin{align*}
\beta_{t,x}^s &= \ln \zeta^s_{t,x} \\
&= \ln d_{t,x}^s - \ln \alpha_{n,x}^s \\
&= \ln d_{t,x}^s - \frac{1}{n}\sum^n_{t=1}\ln d_{t,x}^s,
\end{align*}
where $\bm{\beta}_{x}^s = (\beta_{1, x}^s,\dots,\beta_{n, x}^s)^{\top}$ denotes a set of unconstrained functional time series.
\item By applying eigendecomposition to $\bm{\beta}_{x}^s$, we can express
\begin{equation*}
\beta_{t, x}^{s} = \sum^{L_s}_{\ell=1}\gamma_{t, \ell}\phi_{\ell, x}^{s} + \omega_{t, x}^{s},
\end{equation*}
where $\phi_{\ell, x}^{s}$ is the $\ell$\textsuperscript{th} estimated principal component for age $x$, $\gamma^s_{t,\ell} = \langle \beta_{t, x}^{s}, \phi_{\ell, x}^{s}\rangle$ is the $\ell$\textsuperscript{th} estimated principal component score at time $t$, $\omega_{t, x}^{s}$ denotes the model residuals for age $x$ in year $t$, and $L_{s}$ denotes the number of retained principal components.  For selecting the number of components $L_s$, we implement an eigenvalue ratio criterion of \cite{LRS20}, as well as $L_{s}=6$ recommended in \cite{HBY13}.

\item By conditioning on the estimated principal components and observed data, the $h$-step-ahead forecast of $\beta_{n+h, x}^{s}$ can be estimated by
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{\beta}^{s}_{n+h|n, x} = \sum^{L_s}_{\ell=1}\widehat{\gamma}^s_{n+h|n, \ell}\phi_{\ell, x}^{s},
\end{equation*}
where $\widehat{\gamma}_{n+h|n,\ell}$ denotes the $h$-step-ahead forecast of the principal component scores.
\item By taking the inverse CLR transformation, we traverse back to the original simplex
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{\zeta}_{n+h|n,x}^s = \exp^{\widehat{\beta}^s_{n+h|n,x}}.
\end{equation*}
\item Then, we add back the geometric mean to obtain the forecasts of the life-table death count $d_{n+h, x}^{s}$:
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{d}_{n+h|n, x}^{s} = \widehat{\zeta}_{n+h|n, x}^{s}\times \alpha_{n, x}^{s}.
\end{equation*}
\end{asparaenum}

\subsection{Cumulative distribution function transformation}\label{sec:3.2}

For a time series of CDFs, it is important to scale the life-table death counts to sum to one. We describe the transformation in the following steps:
\begin{asparaenum}[1)]
\item Compute the empirical cumulative distribution function via the cumulative sum,
\begin{equation*}
D^s_{t,x} = \sum^x_{i=1}d^s_{t,i}, \quad x=1,\dots,111,\quad t=1,\dots,n,
\end{equation*}
where $D^s_{t,111} = 1$. 
\item Perform the logistic transformation,
\begin{equation*}
Z^s_{t,x} = \text{logit}(D^s_{t,x}) = \ln\left(\frac{D^s_{t,x}}{1-D^s_{t,x}}\right),
\end{equation*}
where $\ln(\cdot)$ denotes the natural logarithm.
\item With a set of unconstrained data $\bm{Z}^{s}_{x} = (Z^{s}_{1,x}, \dots, Z^{s}_{n,x})^{\top}$, we implement a univariate functional time-series forecasting method. By computing the sample variance, we express a stochastic process $Z^{s}_{t,x}$ as
\begin{equation*}
Z^{s}_{t,x} = \sum^{K_{s}}_{k=1}\eta^{s}_{t, k}\psi^{s}_{k, x}+\varepsilon^{s}_{t, x},
\end{equation*}
where $\psi^{s}_{k, x}$ denotes the $k$\textsuperscript{th} principal component for age $x$ and sex $s$, $\eta^{s}_{t, k} = \langle Z^{s}_{t, x}, \psi^{s}_{k, x}\rangle$ is the estimated principal component score at time $t$, $\varepsilon^{s}_{t, x}$ denotes the model residual function for sex $s$ in year $t$, and $K_{s}$ denotes the number of retained principal components. We implement an eigenvalue ratio criterion of \cite{LRS20}, as well as $K_{s}=6$ recommended in \cite{HBY13}.
\item By conditioning on the estimated functional principal components $\bm{\Psi}^{s}_{x} = (\psi_{1, x},\dots,\psi_{K_{s}, x})$ and observed data $\bm{Z}_{x}^{s}=(Z_{1, x}^{s}, \dots, Z_{n, x}^{s})$, the $h$-step-ahead forecast of $Z_{n+h, x}^{s}$ can be obtained
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{Z}_{n+h|n, x}^{s} = \text{E}[Z_{n+h, x}^{s}|\bm{\Psi}^{s}_{x}, \bm{Z}_{x}^{s}] = \sum^{K_{s}}_{k=1}\widehat{\eta}_{n+h|n, k}^{s}\psi_{k, x}^{s},
\end{equation*}
where $\widehat{\eta}_{n+h|n, k}^{s}$ denotes the $h$-step-ahead univariate time-series forecast of the principal component scores. Among the univariate time-series methods, we consider the exponential smoothing method of \cite{HKO+08} to model and forecast each set of scores.
\item By taking the inverse logit transformation, we obtain
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{D}_{n+h|n, x}^{s} = \frac{\exp^{\widehat{Z}_{n+h|n, x}^{s}}}{1+\exp^{\widehat{Z}_{n+h|n, x}^{s}}}.
\end{equation*}
\item By taking the first-order differencing, we obtain
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{d}^{s}_{n+h|n, x} = \Delta^{x}_{i=1}\widehat{D}^{s}_{n+h|n, i},
\end{equation*}
where $\Delta$ represents the first-order differencing.
\end{asparaenum}

\section{Forecast accuracy comparison}\label{sec:4}

\subsection{Expanding window}\label{sec:4.1}

We implement an expanding-window scheme, which is commonly used to evaluate model and parameter stability, as well as prediction accuracy over time. The expanding window analysis determines the stability of a model's parameter estimates and their forecasts over an expanding window through the sample. Since each data set has a different sample period, we keep the last 20 years of data for evaluation and the remaining data for training. For the 20 years of data in the testing sample, we consider one-step-ahead to 20-step-ahead forecasts to assess the model's performance at the short to medium horizons. Through the expanding-window scheme, we produce 20 one-step-ahead forecasts, 19 two-step-ahead forecasts, $\dots$, and one 20-step-ahead forecast. We compare these forecasts with the holdout data in the testing sample to determine the accuracy of the out-of-sample forecast. In Figure~\ref{fig:2}, we present a concept diagram.
