Via a standard life-table calculation, we convert the forecasts of life-table death counts $(d_x)$ (from 2022 to 2071) to their corresponding survival probabilities ($p_x$). We plug the age-specific survival probabilities into the calculation of single-premium term immediate annuities, and we adopt a \textit{cohort} approach to calculating the survival probabilities, as is the practice of insurance companies, that is, survival probability $p_{60}$ is computed from the forecasted life-table death counts in 2022, $p_{61}$ is computed from the forecasted life-table death counts in 2023, $\dots$, $p_{109}$ is computed from the forecasted life-table death counts in 2071. The $\tau$ year survival probability of a person with entry age~$x$ at the contract time is determined by
\begin{equation*}
_{\tau}p_x = \prod^{\tau}_{j=1}p_{x+j-1} = \prod^{\tau}_{j=1}(1-q_{x+j-1}) = \prod^{\tau}_{j=1}(1-\frac{d_{x+j-1}}{l_{x+j-1}}),
\end{equation*}
where $d_{x+j-1}$ denotes the number of death counts between ages $x+j-1$ and $x+j$, and $l_{x+j-1}$ represents the number of lives alive at age $x+j-1$.

Once we compute the survival probabilities for ages at and beyond 60, the calculation of annuity prices also depends on the value of the zero-coupon bond price. For an $x$-year-old with a benefit, one monetary amount per year is given by
\begin{equation*}
a_x^{T} = \sum^{T}_{\tau=1}B(0,\tau)_{\tau}p_{x},
\end{equation*}
where $B(0,\tau)$ is the $\tau$-year bond price and $_{\tau}p_x$ denotes the survival probability.

