\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:6}

For modeling and forecasting age distribution of death counts, we consider the centered log-ratio and cumulative distribution function transformations. Both transformations allow us to bring a constrained object to an unconstrained space, where the linear techniques can be applied. Such a technique is principal component analysis, which can summarize and model variation in the unconstrained data. Using 24 countries' data, we evaluate the point forecast accuracy (measured by the Kullback-Leibler and Jensen-Shannon divergences) and the interval forecast accuracy (measured by the coverage probability difference between empirical and nominal coverage probabilities and mean interval score). For producing both point and interval forecasts, the cumulative distribution function transformation provides smaller point and interval forecast errors than the centered log-ratio transformation. Between the two ways of selecting the number of principal components, we advocate the selection of $K=6$ for forecasting. 

We apply the cumulative distribution function transformation to generate 50-years-ahead forecasts of age-specific life-table death counts for 24 countries. From the forecasted age distribution of death counts, we evaluate and compare the life expectancy at birth across multiple countries. From the forecasted life-table death counts, we convert them into age-specific survival probabilities and obtain temporary annuity prices. As expected, we observe that the survival probability has a pronounced impact on annuity prices. Coupling with the uncertainty in interest rates, we quantify the uncertainty of annuity prices via pointwise prediction intervals. For reproducibility, the computer \Rlogo \ code is available at \url{https://github.com/hanshang/CLR_vs_CDF_transformation}.

There are a few ways in which the methodology presented in the paper could be extended, and we briefly discuss two. First, although we calculate temporary annuities, other types of annuity prices, such as the whole-life immediate annuity or deferred annuity, are possible. Second, we could consider cohort life-table death counts for modeling a group of individuals, such as baby boomers.

