\section{Introduction}

	In \cite{Paolini_BLMS}, inspired by a descriptive set theoretic question from \cite{coarse}, we proved that, under the assumption of weak elimination of imaginaries, we have that the open subgroups of a countable $\omega$-categorical structure $M$ admit a combinatorial characterization as ``generalized setwise stabilizers'' of finitely generated Galois algebraically closed sets of $M$. This led to a partial solution to one of the problems posed in \cite{coarse}, and also, more algebraically, to a concrete description of the group $\mrm{Aut}_{\mrm{top}}(G)$ of topological automorphisms of $G = \mrm{Aut}(M)$, where on $G$ we consider the topology induced by the usual topology of finite information on $\mrm{Sym}(\omega)$. The point of this paper is to extend the techniques introduced in \cite{Paolini_BLMS} (and later also explored in \cite{coarse_PE}) outside of the context of $\omega$-categorical structures, with connections to classical works of Evans $\&$ Lascar on outer (topological) automorphisms of groups of automorphisms of countable $\omega$-saturated models of strongly minimal theories.


	From now on, all the structures $M$ considered in this paper will be countable and in a countable language. Also, we will denote by $\mathbf{A}(M)$ the set of Galois algebraically closed sets of $M$ of the form $\mrm{acl}^{\mrm{g}}_M(A)$ for $A$ a finite subset of $M$, where ``Galois algebraic closure'' is as in Galois theory, cf. \ref{algebraicity}. 
%
	As well known, for $\omega$-categorical structures $M$, the hypothesis of weak elimination of imaginaries admits many equivalent definitions, the one that will be relevant to our paper, and which was crucial for \cite{Paolini_BLMS}, is the following: for every open subgroup $H$ of $G = \mrm{Aut}(M)$ there is a (unique) $K \in \mathbf{A}(M)$ such that $G_{(K)} \leq H \leq G_{\{K\}}$, where $G_{(K)}$ and $G_{\{K\}}$ denote ``pointwise stabilizer'' and ``setwise stabilizer'', respectively. This condition arose naturally also outside of the $\omega$-categorical context, and it appeared elsewhere in the literature, in particular in the works of Poizat \cite[Chapter 16]{poizat} on one side, and Evans and Lascar \cite{Evans_ACF} on the other side. We will see that this condition, together with a natural saturation assumption, allows for the techniques from \cite{Paolini_BLMS} to be applied outside of the context of $\omega$-categorical structures (while at the same time subsuming the $\omega$-categorical context). We isolate these two conditions in the following definition:

	\begin{definition}\label{hyp_intro} We say that $M$ has the Lascar Property if it satisfies the following:
	\begin{enumerate}[(1)]	
	\item\label{hyp_1b} for every $K,S \in \mathbf{A}(M)$, if $S \nsubseteq K$, then there is $g \in G_{(K)}$ and $h \in G_{(S)}$ such that:
	$$g^{-1}hg(S) \neq S;$$
	\item\label{hyp_2b} for every open $H \leq G = \mrm{Aut}(M)$, there is $K \in \mathbf{A}(M)$ such that:
$$G_{(K)} \leq H \leq G_{\{K\}}.$$
	\end{enumerate}
\end{definition} 

	As we shall see, in the context of (infinite) $\omega$-categorical structures, the assumption \ref{hyp_intro}(\ref{hyp_1b}) is automatically satisfied, while the assumption \ref{hyp_intro}(\ref{hyp_2b}) corresponds {\em exactly} to weak elimination of imaginaries. This is the context of the aforementioned reference \cite{Paolini_BLMS}. The next theorem shows that the analysis undertaken in \cite{Paolini_BLMS} generalizes smoothly to the general context of countable structures satisfying \ref{hyp_intro}, and so in particular the requirement of local finiteness of the Galois algebraic closure operator (which is satisfied in the $\omega$-categorical context) is not necessary.

	\begin{theorem}\label{main_th} Let $M, N$ be with the Lascar \mbox{property (cf. \ref{hyp_intro}) and $G = \mrm{Aut}(M)$.}
	\begin{enumerate}[(1)]
	\item Pointwise stabilizers of finitely generated algebraically closed subsets of $M$ can be characterized, and this determines a definable Galois correspondence between such $G_\delta$-subgroups of $G$ and finitely generated algebraically closed subsets of $M$;
	\item We can associate to $M$ a canonical first-order structure $\mathcal{E}^{\mrm{ex}}_M$, essentially made of finitely generated algebraically closed subsets of $M$ with partial isomorphisms of $M$ between such sets, so that we have: $\mrm{Aut}(M) \cong_{\mrm{top}} \mrm{Aut}(N) \; \Leftrightarrow \; \mathcal{E}^{\mrm{ex}}_M \cong \mathcal{E}^{\mrm{ex}}_N$.
	\item In case $M = N$ we have that $\mrm{Aut}_{\mrm{top}}(G)$ is naturally isomorphic to $\mrm{Aut}(\mathcal{E}^{\mrm{ex}}_M)$.
	\end{enumerate}
	\end{theorem}

	In many respects, Theorem~\ref{main_th} can be considered best possible in terms of describing topological isomorphisms, and in fact in many cases of interest one can only look at a slice, or a finite number of slices, of $\mathcal{E}^{\mrm{ex}}_M$ (see \ref{main_theorem} for a precise statement), and all this allows for a concrete characterization of $\mrm{Aut}_{\mrm{top}}(\mrm{Aut}(M))$. In fact, we will see that \ref{main_th} has a number of explicit applications, but before we move on to applications, it is natural to ask: when are our assumptions from \ref{hyp_intro} satisfied? We dwell on this question in Section~\ref{section4}. We will prove there that the existence of an integer-valued rank function inducing a stationary independence relation which admits weak canonical bases (in the sense of \ref{def_WCB}), together with a Noetherianity condition on $\mathbf{A}(M)$, suffices to have the Lascar Property (cf. \ref{prop_TPP} and \ref{th_Lascar_baguette}). This subsumes various cases of interest and in particular several classical structures of finite Morley rank, most notably, the theory of infinite $\mathbb{K}$-vector spaces (for $\mathbb{K}$ a countable field) and the theory $\mrm{ACF_0}$ of algebraically closed fields of characteristic~$0$.

    We then finally arrive at the announced applications of our methods. In \cite{Evans_ACF} Evans $\&$ Lascar proved that the automorphism group of the countable saturated model of $\mrm{ACF}_0$ is complete (i.e., the group has a trivial center and every automorphism is inner). In \cite{konnerth} Konnerth proved that the outer automorphism group of the automorphism group of the countable saturated model of $\mrm{DCF}_0$ consists of the multiplicative group of the rationals. We will use our technology to generalize their proofs in a unifying framework. Our main tool in this respect is the following:

	\begin{theorem}\label{genevansth} Let $M$ be a countable structure with the Lascar Property (cf \ref{hyp_intro}) and suppose that $\mathbb{G}_M = (M,\mrm{acl}_M^{\mrm{g}})$ is a pregeometry. Then there exists a canonical map $\pi$ from $\mrm{Aut}_{\mrm{top}}(\mrm{Aut}(M))$ into $\mrm{Aut}(\mathbb{G}_M)$. Suppose further there is $\mrm{Aut}(M)\leq H \leq \mrm{Sym}(\omega)$ such that for every $g \in \mrm{Aut}(\mathbb{G}_M)$ there exists $\hat{g} \in H$ satisfying:
    \begin{enumerate}[(1)]
    \item for every $A \in \mathbf{A}(M)$ and $h \in H$, $h(A) \in \mathbf{A}(M)$; and the map $A \mapsto h(A)$ induces a homomorphism $\phi_H$  from $H$ onto $\mrm{Aut}(\mathbb{G}_M)$; 
            \item for every $h \in H$, $h(\cdot)h^{-1} \in \mrm{Aut}_{\mrm{top}}(\mrm{Aut}(M))$;
    		\item the map $g \mapsto \hat{g}$ is a homomorphism;
        \item $\pi(\hat{g}(\cdot)\hat{g}^{-1})=g$.
    \end{enumerate}
    In fact, more precisely, we have that the following diagram commutes:
    $$\begin{tikzcd}
    H \arrow[r, "\phi_H"] \arrow[d, "\gamma"]
    & \mrm{Aut}(\mathbb{G}_M) \\
    \mrm{Aut}_{\mrm{top}}(\mrm{Aut}(M)) \arrow{ur}{\pi} 
    &
    \end{tikzcd}$$ where $\gamma$ is conjugation; and $\mrm{Aut}_{\mrm{top}}(\mrm{Aut}(M))\cong \mrm{ker}(\pi) \rtimes \mrm{Aut}(\mathbb{G}_M)$.
    \end{theorem}

    This allows us to readily infer the aforementioned results of Evans $\&$ Lascar and of Konnerth. Notice that it is known that in the case of $\mrm{ACF}_0$ we also have the so-called small index property (i.e., every open subgroup of countable index is open), and so in this case $\mrm{Aut}_{\mrm{top}}(\mrm{Aut}(M)) = \mrm{Aut}(\mrm{Aut}(M))$, i.e., every automorphism of $\mrm{Aut}(M)$ is automatically topological (this is why we can drop ``$\mrm{top}$'' below).

    \begin{corollary}\label{acf_corollary} Let $M\models \mrm{ACF}_0$ be countable and saturated, then we have:
    $$\mrm{Aut}(\mrm{Aut}(M))\cong \mrm{Aut}(\mathbb{G}_M),$$
and automorphisms of $\mrm{Aut}(M)$ are \mbox{inner (so $\mrm{Aut}(M)$, being centerless, is complete).}
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}\label{dcf_corollary} Let $M \models \mrm{DCF}_0$ be countable and saturated, then we have:
$$\mrm{Aut}_{\mrm{top}}(\mrm{Aut}(M))\cong \mrm{Aut}(\mathbb{G}_M).$$
\end{corollary}

    Finally, we use \ref{genevansth} to give a description of $\mrm{Aut}(\mrm{Aut}(M))$ in the context of vector spaces, this last result generalizes the analogous result from \cite{Paolini_BLMS} in the $\omega$-categorical context. At the best of our knowledge, this last application is new, but proofs using different methods might have appeared elsewhere in the literature. Notice that also in this case it is known that the small index property holds (so we can drop ``$\mrm{top}$'').

	\begin{corollary}\label{vectspace_corollary} Let $\mathbb{K}$ be a countable field and let $T_\mathbb{K}$ be the theory of infinite $\mathbb{K}$-vector spaces. Let $M \models T_\mathbb{K}$ be countable and saturated, then we have:
	$$\mrm{Aut}(\mrm{Aut}(M)) \cong \mrm{Aut}(\mathbb{K}^\times) \rtimes \mrm{Aut}(\mathbb{G}_M).$$
\end{corollary}

    Finally, we observe that the scope of applicability of our methods is wider than one might expect from a first look, if one is willing to pass from $M$ to $M^{\mrm{eq}}$. By this we mean that although $M$ may fail \ref{hyp_intro}(\ref{hyp_2}), adding additional sorts from $M^{\mrm{eq}}$, we might very well make \ref{hyp_intro}(\ref{hyp_2}) true. This is most easily seen in the case of $\omega$-categorical structures (where condition \ref{hyp_intro}(\ref{hyp_2}) corresponds {\em exactly} to weak elimination of imaginaries), but this remark is \mbox{meaningful also outside of the $\omega$-categorical context.}

    Concerning the structure of our paper. In Section~\ref{char_stab} we characterize pointwise stabilizers. In Section~\ref{sec_reconstruction} we prove Theorem~\ref{main_th}. In Section~\ref{section4} we explore when the conditions from \ref{hyp_intro} are satisfied. Finally, in Section~\ref{sec_applications} we focus on applications.

\section{Characterizing pointwise stabilizers}\label{char_stab}

In this section we provide a characterization of the pointwise stabilizers of finitely generated Galois-algebraically closed subsets of a countable structure $M$ satisfying the Lascar Property. This result is essential for the proofs presented in the following sections. First of all, in the next two items we introduce the basic definitions from permutation group theory that we need.

\smallskip

	\begin{notation} 
	\begin{enumerate}[(1)]
	\item We write $A \subseteq_\omega M$ to denote finite subsets.
	\item Given a structure $M$ and $A \subseteq M$, and considering $\mrm{Aut}(M) = G$ in its natural action on $M$, we denote the pointwise (resp. setwise) stabilizer of $A$ under this action by $G_{(A)}$ (resp. $G_{\{ A \}}$). 
	\item We denote the subgroup relation by $\leq$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{notation}

\begin{definition}\label{algebraicity} Let $M$ be a structure and $G = \mrm{Aut}(M)$.
	\begin{enumerate}[(1)]
	\item $a \in M$ is Galois-algebraic  over $A \subseteq M$ if the orbit of $a$ under $G_{(A)}$, denoted as $\mathcal{O}(a/A)$, is finite. 
	\item The {\em Galois-algebraic closure} of $A \subseteq M$, denoted as $\mrm{acl}^{\mrm{g}}_M(A)$, is the set of elements of $M$ which are Galois-algebraic over $A$.
%	\item $a \in M$ is Galois-definable  over $A \subseteq M$ if the orbit of $a$ under $G_{(A)}$ is $\{a\}$. 
%	\item The {\em Galois-definable closure} of $A \subseteq M$ in $M$, denoted as $\mrm{dcl}^{\mrm{g}}_M(A)$, is the set of elements of $M$ which are Galois-definable over $A$. 
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}
\begin{notation} \label{algclosednotation}
	\begin{enumerate}[(1)]
	\item We let $\mathbf{A}(M) = \{ \mrm{acl}^{\mrm{g}}_M(A) : A \subseteq_{\omega} M \}$.
        \item For $K \in \mathbf{A}(M)$ we denote by $\mrm{Aut}_M(K)$ the set of automorphisms of $K$ which extends to an automorphisms of $M$.
	\item We let $\mathbf{EA}(M) = \{ (K, L) : K \in \mathbf{A}(M) \text{ and } L \in \mathrm{Aut}_M(K) \}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{notation}
%Therefore, when we write $\mrm{Aut}(K)$ we really mean all the automorphisms of $K$ that extend to automorphisms of $M$.

%In the next hypothesis, we isolate the abstract properties of topological groups that make our ``reconstruction proof'' work. We could have worked at the less general level of topological groups directly, but we hope (and believe) that the added generality might be relevant for other possible applications of our methods.
	\begin{hypothesis}\label{hyp} Throughout this section, let $M$ be countable and such that:
	\begin{enumerate}[(1)]	
	\item\label{hyp_1} for every $K,S \in \mathbf{A}(M)$, if $S \nsubseteq K$, then there is $g \in G_{(K)}$ and $h \in G_{(S)}$ such that:
	$$g^{-1}hg(S) \neq S;$$
	\item\label{hyp_2} for every open $H \leq G = \mrm{Aut}(M)$, there is $K \in \mathbf{A}(M)$ such that:
$$G_{(K)} \leq H \leq G_{\{K\}}.$$
	\end{enumerate}
\end{hypothesis} 

\begin{proposition}\label{unique_K}
    For every open subgroup  $H$ of $G$ there is a unique $K \in \mathbf{A}(M)$ such that the hypothesis \ref{hyp}(\ref{hyp_2}) holds.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    Let $K_1,K_2\in \mathbf{A}(M)$ such that for $i=1,2$ we have $$G_{(K_i)} \leq H \leq G_{\{K_i\}}.$$
    We want to prove that $K_1=K_2$. We show the left to right inclusion; the other one is identical.
    By the two sandwiches, we have 
    $$G_{(K_1)} \leq H \leq G_{\{K_2\}}.$$
    Suppose $K_2 \nsubseteq K_1$ then by \ref{hyp}(\ref{hyp_1}) there are $g \in G_{(K_1)}$ and $h \in G_{(K_2)}$ such that $g^{-1}hg(K_2) \neq K_2$. The automorphism $h \in G_{(K_2)} \subseteq G_{\{K_2\}}$ and $g \in G_{(K_1)} \subseteq G_{\{K_2\}}$ by the sandwich, but $g^{-1}hg \notin G_{\{K_2\}}$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}
For this reason (i.e., \ref{unique_K}), following \cite{konnerth}, given an open subgroup  $H$ of $G$, we call the unique $K \in \mathbf{A}(M)$ from \ref{unique_K} the support of $H$.
%	The next definition is one of a technical nature, but an important one, in fact we need this for the statement of the main technical result of this paper, i.e., \ref{the_first_crucial_lemma}.

In the next definition we introduce one of the most important definitions of the paper, we refer to the objects we introduce in \ref{GS_def} as {\em generalized pointwise stabilizers}.

\begin{definition}\label{GS_def} Let $(K, L) \in \mathbf{EA}(M)$, we define:
	$$ G_{(K, L)} = \{ f \in \mrm{Aut}(M) : f(K) = K \text{ and } f \restriction K \in L \}.$$
\end{definition}

Notice that if $L = \{ \mrm{id}_K \}$, then  $G_{(K, L)} = G_{(K)}$, i.e., it equals the pointwise stabilizer of $K$, and that if $L = \mrm{Aut}_M(K)$, then $G_{(K, L)} = G_{\{ K \}}$ (c.f. \ref{algclosednotation}(2)), i.e., it equals the setwise stabilizer of $K$.
%
That is why we referred to $ G_{(K, L)}$ as generalized pointwise stabilizers (and why we use $\mathcal{GS}(M)$ below). We introduce the following notation:
$$\mathcal{PS}(M) = \{ G_{(K)} : K \in \mathbf{A}(M) \} \; \text{ and } \; \mathcal{GS}(M) =\{ G_{(K, L)} : (K, L) \in \mathbf{EA}(M) \}.$$

\begin{lemma}\label{baguettelemma} If $H \leq G$ is such that $G_{(K)} \leq H \leq G_{\{K\}}$ for a certain $K \in \mathbf{A}(M)$, then $H \in \mathcal{GS}(M)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} Firstly, we claim that $G_{(K)} \trianglelefteq G_{\{ K \}}$. In fact, for $g \in G_{\{ K \}}$, $h \in G_{(K)}$ and $a \in K$, we have $ghg^{-1}(a) = gg^{-1}(a) = a$, since $g^{-1}(a) \in K$ and $h \in G_{(K)}$. Secondly, for $g, h \in G_{\{ K \}}$, we have $g^{-1}h \in G_{(K)}$ iff $g \restriction K = h \restriction K$. 
Hence,the map $f:gG_{(K)} \mapsto g\restriction K$, for $g \in G_{\{K\}}$ is such that:
\begin{equation*}\tag{$\star_1$}\label{equation_label}
    f:G_{\{K\}}/G_{(K)} \cong \mrm{Aut}_M(K)
\end{equation*}
since every automorphism in  $\mrm{Aut}_M(K)$ extends to an automorphism of $M$ (c.f. \ref{algclosednotation}(2)). Thus, by the fourth isomorphism theorem, $H=G_{(K,L)}$, for $L=\{f\restriction K : f \in H\}$.
\end{proof}

Therefore, recalling \ref{hyp}, every open subgroup can be seen as generalized pointwise stabilizer. 

In the next two propositions, we characterize the notion of subgroup and normal subgroup relations between generalized pointwise stabilizers. Besides being interesting in their own right, they are crucial for achieving our objective.

\begin{proposition}\label{pre_normality_prop} Let $H_1, H_2 \in \mathcal{GS}(M)$. The following conditions are equivalent:
	\begin{enumerate}[(1)]
	\item $H_1 \leq H_2$;
	\item there are $K_1, K_2 \in \mathbf{A}(M)$ and $L_i \leq \mrm{Aut}_M(K_i)$ such that:
	\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
	\item $H_i = G_{(K_i, L_i)}$;
	\item $K_2 \subseteq K_1$;
	\item for every $f \in L_1$, $f(K_2) = K_2$ and $f \restriction K_2 \in L_2$.	\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Concerning (2) implies (1), suppose not, and let $h \in H_1 \setminus H_2$. Since $K_2 \subseteq K_1$, $h \restriction K_1 \in L_1$ but $(h\restriction K_1) \restriction K_2=h\restriction K_2 \notin L_2$, a contradiction.

\noindent Now we show that (1) implies (2).
\begin{enumerate}[$(\star_2)$]
	\item $K_2 \subseteq K_1$.
\end{enumerate}
Suppose not, by \ref{hyp}(\ref{hyp_1}) there is $g \in G_{(K_1)}$ and $h \in G_{(K_2)}$ such that $g^{-1}hg(K_2) \neq K_2$. The automorphism $g \in G_{(K_1)}\subseteq H_1$ but $g \notin G_{\{K_2\}}$, and so $g \notin H_2$, a contradiction.
\begin{enumerate}[$(\star_3)$]
	\item for every $f \in L_1$, $f(K_2) = K_2$ and $f \restriction K_2 \in L_2$.
\end{enumerate}
Let $f \in L_1$ and  $\hat{f}\in \mrm{Aut}(M)$ his expansion. Suppose $f \restriction K_2 \notin L_2$ or $f(K_2)\neq K_2$, then $\hat{f} \notin H_2$. Therefore $\hat{f} \in H_1 \setminus H_2$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{normality_prop} Let $H_1, H_2 \in \mathcal{GS}(M)$. The following conditions are equivalent:
	\begin{enumerate}[(1)]
	\item $H_1 \trianglelefteq H_2$;
	\item there is $K \in \mathbf{A}(M)$ and $L_1 \trianglelefteq L_2 \leq \mrm{Aut}_M(K)$ such that $H_i = G_{(K, L_i)}$ for $i =1,2$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

	\begin{proof} The proof of (2) implies (1) is immediate, since by the normality of $L_1$ in $L_2$ we have that, for $g \in G_{(K, L_2)}$ and $h \in G_{(K, L_1)}$, $g h g^{-1} \restriction K \in L_1$.

\noindent We show that (1) implies (2). By assumption, $H_i = G_{(K_i, L_i)}$ for $(K_i, L_i) \in \mathbf{EA}(M)$ ($i =1,2$). 
\begin{enumerate}[$(\star_4)$]
    \item $K_2 \subseteq K_1$.
\end{enumerate}
By hypothesis, $H_1 \leq H_2$, so for the Proposition \ref{pre_normality_prop} the thesis. 
\begin{enumerate}[$(\star_5)$]
	\item $K_1 \subseteq K_2$.
\end{enumerate}
Suppose not, by \ref{hyp}(\ref{hyp_1}) there is $g \in G_{(K_2)}$ and $h \in G_{(K_1)}$ such that $g^{-1}hg(K_1) \neq K_1$. The automorphism $h \in G_{(K_1)} \subseteq H_1$ and $g \in G_{(K_2)} \subseteq H_2$ but $g^{-1}hg \notin G_{\{K_1\}}$, and so $g \notin H_1$, a contradiction.
\begin{enumerate}[$(\star_6)$]
	\item $L_1 \leq L_2$.
\end{enumerate}
Suppose not, and let $h \in L_1 \setminus L_2$. Then, recalling \ref{algclosednotation}(2), $h$ extends to an automorphism $\hat{h}$ of $M$. Clearly $\hat{h} \in H_1 \setminus H_2$, a contradiction.
\begin{enumerate}[$(\star_7)$]
	\item $L_1 \trianglelefteq L_2$.
\end{enumerate}
Suppose not, and let $g_i \in L_i$ ($i = 1, 2$) be such that $g_2g_1g_2^{-1} \not\in L_1$. Then, by \ref{algclosednotation}(2), $g_i$ extends to an automorphism $\hat{g}_i$ of $M$ ($i = 1, 2$). Clearly $\hat{g}_i \in H_i$ ($i = 1, 2$), and $\hat{g}_2\hat{g}_1\hat{g}_2^{-1} \not\in H_1$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition} Let: $$\mathcal{OL}(M)=\{H\leq G: H=\bigcap_{i \in I}H_i \text{ with } H_{i+1}\trianglelefteq H_i \text{ and } H_i\in \Sigma^0_1 \text{ for all } i \in I \text{ countable } \}$$
Then $\mathcal{OL}(M) \subseteq \mathcal{GS}(M)$. 
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
    Let $H \in \mathcal{OL}$. Then $H=\bigcap_{i <\omega}H_i$ with $H_i$ open subgroups for every $ i \in \omega$. By Lemma \ref{baguettelemma} for every $i \in \omega $ exists $(K_i,L_i)\in \mathbf{EA}(M)$  such that $H_i=G_{(K_i,L_i)}$. Since $H_{i+1} \trianglelefteq H_i$, by Proposition \ref{normality_prop}, $K_i=K_0$ for every $i \in \omega$. We claim that $H=G_{(K_0,L)}$ with $L=\{f \restriction K_0 : f \in G,f(K_0)= K_0 \text{ and } f\restriction K_0 \in L_i \text{ for every } i < \omega \}$. Let $f \in H$. Since $f \in G_{(K_0,L_i)}$, $f(K_0)=K_0$, $f\restriction K_0 \in L_i$ and that is $f \in G_{(K_0,L)}$. For the other way, suppose not and let $f \in G_{(K_0,L)} \setminus H$. Then $f \notin G_{(K_0,L_i)}$ for a certain $i <\omega$. So $f(K_0)\neq K_0$ or $f\restriction K_0 \notin L_i$, that is $f \notin L$, a contradiction. 
\end{proof}

The idea behind $\mathcal{OL}$ is to consider only intersections of open sets with the same support. To do so, we consider intersections of normal chain of open sets. For this reason $\mathcal{OL}$ stands for ``open limit''.

\begin{proposition}\label{PSinLO} $\mathcal{PS}(M)\subseteq \mathcal{OL}(M)$. 
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    Let $K \in \mathbf{A}(M)$, i.e., there exists $A \subseteq_\omega M$ such that $K=\mrm{acl}^\mrm{g}_M(A)$. We want to define an increasing chain $\{A_i\}_{i \in I}$ such that the respective stabilizers form a decreasing chain  of open subgroups. Let $\{b_i\}_{i \in I}$ a countable enumeration of $K$. We set $A_0=A$. Since $b_0 \in K$, the orbit $\mathcal{O}(b_0/A)$ is finite. Let $A_1=A_0\cup \mathcal{O}(b_0/A)$. Therefore $G_{(A_1)} \in \Sigma^0_1$ and $G_{(A_1)} \trianglelefteq G_{(A_0)}$. In the same way, we can define $A_{i+1}=A_i \cup \mathcal{O}(b_i/A)$ and we have $G_{(A_{i+1})} \in \Sigma^0_1$ and $G_{(A_{i+1})}\trianglelefteq G_{(A_i)}$. Clearly, $G_{(K)}=\bigcap_{i \in I}  G_{(A_i)}$, i.e.,  $G_{(K)} \in \mathcal{OL}(M)$.
\end{proof}

The next proposition is the core of this section, providing a characterization of the pointwise stabilizers.

	\begin{proposition}\label{char_point_stab} Let: 
	$$\mathcal{G}_2 = \{ H \in \mathcal{OL}(M) : \not\!\exists H' \in \mathcal{OL}(M) \text{ with } H' \subsetneq H \text{ and } H' \trianglelefteq H  \}.$$ Then $\mathcal{PS}(M) = \mathcal{G}_2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
    This proposition can be stated with $\mathcal{GS}(M)$ in place of $\mathcal{OL}(M)$ and the proof is identical, but for our purposes, it is not necessary.  
\end{remark}

	\begin{proof} First we show the containment from left to right. Let $H_2 \in \mathcal{PS}(M)$ and assume that there exists $H_1 \in \mathcal{OL}(M)$ such that $H_1 \subsetneq H_2 \text{ and } H_1 \trianglelefteq H_2$. By Proposition \ref{normality_prop}, $H_i = G_{(K_i, L_i)}$ for $(K_i, L_i) \in \mathbf{EA}(M)$ ($i =1,2$) and $K_1 = K = K_2$. Now, as $H_2 \in \mathcal{PS}(M)$, $L_2 = \{ \mrm{id}_K \}$. Hence, $L_1 = L_2$, and so $H_1 = H_2$, a contradiction. We now show the containment from right to left. Let $H \in \mathcal{G}_2$, then $H = G_{(K, L)}$ for $(K, L) \in \mathbf{EA}(M)$. If $L \neq \{ \mrm{id}_K \}$ then letting $H' = G_{(K, \{ \mrm{id}_K \})}$ we have $H' \subsetneq H$ and $H' \trianglelefteq H$, a contradiction since $H' \in \mathcal{OL}(M)$ by Proposition~\ref{PSinLO}.
\end{proof}

