%Furthermore, reversing the role of $\alpha$ and $\alpha^{-1}$ we also see that:
%$$(f(A), f(B_1), ..., f(B_n)) \in P^{\mathcal{E}^{\mrm{ex}}_N(k)}_n \Rightarrow A \subseteq \mrm{acl}^{\mrm{g}}_M(B_1 \cup \cdots B_n).$$
We now show that $f$ preserves also the relations $E_n$, for $1 < n < \omega$, from \ref{def_expanded_12}(\ref{def_expanded_12_binary}). To this extent observe the following sequence of equivalences:
$$((A_1, p_1, B_1), ..., (A_n, p_n, B_n)) \in E^{\mathcal{E}^{\mrm{ex}}_M(k)}_n$$
$$\Updownarrow$$
$$\exists g \in \mrm{Aut}(M) \text{ s.t. for all } i \in [1, n], \,  g(A_i) = B_i \text{ and } g \restriction A_i = g_i.$$
$$\Updownarrow$$
$$g G_{(A_1)}g^{-1} = G_{(B_1)}, \, ... , \, g G_{(A_n)}g^{-1} = G_{(B_n)}$$
