Even for this larger volume, the model is broadly in agreement with the data. 
However, one can also see that the equilateral $\tilde{B}_{\delta \delta E}$ bispectrum does not match beyond $k \sim 0.10 \,h/\text{Mpc}$ for $(\ell_1,\ell_2) = (0,0)$ and $(\ell_1,\ell_2) = (1,1)$ and `folded' configurations of the form $(k,k/2,k/2)$ are also poorly fit (this is also the region where $\tilde{B}_{\delta \delta E}$ has the highest SNR, so it is perhaps not surprising that the model fails there sooner). Observe that the parity-odd multipole $B_{\delta \delta B}^{00}$ is clearly nonzero for the obtuse configurations, but vanishes for equilateral triangles because of symmetries (see Figure 2 in Paper I).
\subsection{One or two shape fields}\label{subsec:twofield}
For bispectra with two tensor fields, to wit $B_{\delta EE}, B_{\delta EB}$ and $B_{\delta BB}$, an additional parameter enters the tree-level model. This contribution was first derived in \cite{vlah_eft1} and labelled as $\alpha_\text{gg}^{1\delta}$ in Paper I. 
