\section{Hyperparameters}
\label{app:hypers}
We experiment with using a weighted sum of three loss terms,
\[
\mathcal{L}_{\rm total} = \lambda_{\rm ce}\mathcal{L}_{\rm ce} + \lambda_{\rm wass}\mathcal{L}_{\rm wass} + \lambda_{\rm mse}\mathcal{L}_{\rm mse},
\]
where $\lambda_{\rm ce}+\lambda_{\rm wass}+\lambda_{\rm mse}=1$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\rm ce}$ is a simple cross-entropy loss (which we average over perturbations in the training set):
\[
\mathcal{L}_{\rm ce} = -\frac{1}{G}\sum_{g=1}^{G} \sum_{b=1}^{B}h_{gb}\log \hat{h}_{gb}.
\]
We perform a coarse grid search (with step size $0.25$) over valid triples $(\lambda_{\rm ce}, \lambda_{\rm wass}, \lambda_{\rm mse})$ and report mean-shift prediction performance for the Replogle K562 cell line in Table~\ref{tab:hist_loss_weights}. The best configuration ($\lambda_{\text{wass}} = 0.75$ and $\lambda_{\text{mse}} = 0.25$) assigns a majority weight to the Wasserstein term and retains the MSE contribution. Adding even a small weight to the cross-entropy loss hurts performance, with the worst configuration assigning all of the weight to it.

