Recall that the point $(1/p,1/q)$ is at the lower boundary of the range \eqref{eq:improvedrange}, so the two Lebesgue exponents satisfy the sub-H\"{o}lder condition \eqref{eq:subduality}.
Let us substitute $x=1/(y+1)$ for $y\in(1,\infty)$; this change of variables was used in \cite{KT17} and \cite{DGIM21}.
An easy computation gives
\[ c_{q}\Big(\frac{1}{y+1}\Big) = q y (y+1)^{-2/p-2} \big( p y^{2/p} + q y^{2/p-1} -q y - p \big), \]
so, from the previous discussion, we know that $\phi\colon(0,\infty)\to\R$ defined as
\[ \phi(y) := p y^{2/p} + q y^{2/p-1} -q y - p \]
has at least two zeros in $(1,\infty)$.
However, by direct computation, we obtain
\[ \phi'(y) = 2y^{2/p-1} + q \Big(\frac{2}{p}-1\Big) y^{2/p-2} - q \]
and
\[ \phi''(y) = \frac{2(2-p)q}{p} y^{2/p-3} \Big(\frac{y}{q}+\frac{1}{p}-1\Big). \]
Denoting
\[ y_0 := q \Big(1-\frac{1}{p}\Big) \stackrel{\eqref{eq:subduality}}{>} 1, \]
we see that $\phi'$ is strictly decreasing on $(0,y_0]$ and strictly increasing on $[y_0,\infty)$.
Since
\[ \phi'(1) = -2q \Big(1-\frac{1}{p}-\frac{1}{q}\Big) \stackrel{\eqref{eq:subduality}}{<} 0 
\quad\text{and}\quad \lim_{y\to\infty} \phi'(y)=\infty, \]
we see that there exists $y_1\in(y_0,\infty)$ such that $\phi$ is strictly decreasing on $[1,y_1]$ and strictly increasing on $[y_1,\infty)$.
Combining this with $\phi(1)=0$, we conclude that $\phi$ has a unique zero in $(1,\infty)$, which contradicts the previous claim about the existence of two zeros.
This contradiction finally establishes \eqref{eq:mainineq}.

