\section{Recursive Tracking with Kalman Filters} \label{sec:tracking}
After estimating the target positions and velocities, the next step is to track each target. The KF continuously blends incoming, noisy sensor readings with a prediction from a motion model to best estimate the target location and motion.
\subsection{Standard Kalman Filter}
The estimator of Sections~II--III delivers noisy snapshots 
\(\mathbf{z}_k=[\hat x,\hat y,\hat v_x,\hat v_y]_k^\top\) at discrete instants \(kT\), where \(T\) is the KF sampling period.  Assuming constant–velocity motion within each interval, the kinematic state evolves as
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{x}_{k+1}= \mathbf{F}\,\mathbf{x}_k+\mathbf{q}_k,
\quad
\mathbf{F} \;=\;
\begin{bmatrix}
I_{2} & T\,I_{2} \\[6pt]
0_{2} & I_{2}
\end{bmatrix},
\label{eq:F}
\end{equation}
where $I_{2}$ is the $2\times 2$ identity matrix, $0_2$ is $2\times 2$ zero matrix, and  \(\mathbf{q}_k\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{Q})\) is process noise.  
The measurement equation is identity,
\(\mathbf{z}_k=\mathbf{H}\mathbf{x}_k+\mathbf{r}_k\), 
\(\mathbf{H}=\mathbf{I}_4\), where \(\mathbf{r}_k\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{R})\).  
The predict–update recursions are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
& \hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k-1} = F\,\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k-1|k-1}, \:
P_{k|k-1} = F\,P_{k-1|k-1}\,F^{\!\top} + Q, \\
& K_k = P_{k|k-1}\,H^{\!\top}\bigl(H\,P_{k|k-1}\,H^{\!\top} + R\bigr)^{-1}, \\
& \hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k} = \hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k-1} + K_k\bigl(\mathbf{z}_k - H\,\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k-1}\bigr), \\
& P_{k|k} = (I - K_k H)\,P_{k|k-1}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}



The standard KF relies on a predefined state-transition model 
$\mathbf{F}$ that must match the true target dynamics (e.g., constant velocity). When the target accelerates or turns, this model mismatch leads to degraded tracking performance.


\subsection{Extended Kalman Filter (EKF)}
Instead of fixed motion models, the state is augmented to \(\mathbf{x}_k = [x_k, y_k, v_k, \theta_k]^\top\) and the nonlinear process for prediction is \(f(\mathbf{x}) = [x + T\, v\cos\theta,\;y + T\,v\sin\theta,\;v,\;\theta]^\top\). The measurement function then maps the augmented state into the observed vector by \(h(\mathbf{x}) = [x,\;y,\;v\cos\theta,\;v\sin\theta]^\top\), and the actual measurement is \(\mathbf{z}_k = h(\mathbf{x}_k) + \mathbf{r}_k\) with \(\mathbf{r}_k\sim\mathcal{N}(0,R)\).



After linearizing the Jacobians, the EKF recursions become
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\hspace{-0.2cm}\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k-1} = f(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k-1|k-1}), P_{k|k-1} = F_k\,P_{k-1|k-1}\,F_k^{\!\top} + Q, \\
&\hspace{-0.2cm}K_k = P_{k|k-1}\,H_k^{\!\top}\bigl(H_k\,P_{k|k-1}\,H_k^{\!\top} + R\bigr)^{-1}, \\
&\hspace{-0.2cm}\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k} = \hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k-1} + K_k\bigl(\mathbf{z}_k - h(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k|k-1})\bigr), \\
&\hspace{-0.2cm}P_{k|k} = (I - K_k H_k)\,P_{k|k-1}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}


By directly measuring speed \(v_k\) and heading \(\theta_k\), the EKF predicts \((x,y)\) along the observed velocity vector and updates  \(v\) and \(\theta\) using Doppler estimates. Process noise on \(v,\theta\) accounts for acceleration and turns, enabling the filter to operate without a fixed motion model and learn the trajectory from the \((x,y,v,\theta)\)  sequence under smoothness constraints.

We consider tracking a single target under linear and circular-type motion profiles. The target proceeds at a steady nominal speed, modulated by small 5\% fluctuations to mimic throttle or wind variations,. In circular motion, it performs a slow, constant‐rate turn that traces a gentle circular-type path such that the true velocity rotates at constant angular rate, causing the KF to correct the occasional outliers produced by the trilateration stage. KF sampling period $T$ is 1\,sec, and the KF noise covariances  are set as $\mathbf{Q}=\operatorname{diag}(10^{-4},10^{-4},10^{-2},10^{-2})$, and $\mathbf{R}=\operatorname{diag}(10,10,1,1)$. For linear motion, the nonlinear state in EKF and repeated linearizations introduce small gain mismatches, whereas the standard KF uses an exact constant-velocity model, which makes it the optimal minimum-variance estimator and slightly more accurate on linear trajectories.


Table~\ref{tab:ekf_results} shows the total measurement and filtering errors under linear and circular motion profiles using the standard KF and EKF. The EKF can effectively track both linear and circular motions, while the standard KF yields slightly more accurate results for the linear trajectory but fails in closely tracking non-linear (circular) motions. Fig.~\ref{fig:tracking_linear_circular_EKF} illustrates the tracks achieved by the EKF under both motion profiles in comparison with measurement results for positioning



