\begin{abstract}

% \shuiwang{1. How to obtain a foundation interatomic potential model that can perform relaxation? 2. How to make use of the foundation model to obtain molecular geometries to be used in downstream tasks?}
% \textcolor{red}{Cong: modified abstract based on Dr.Ji's comments}

Accurate molecular property predictions require 3D geometries, which are typically obtained using expensive methods such as density functional theory (DFT). Here, we attempt to obtain molecular geometries by relying solely on machine learning interatomic potential (MLIP) models.
To this end, we first curate a large-scale molecular relaxation dataset comprising 3.5 million molecules and 300 million snapshots. Then MLIP foundation models are trained with supervised learning to predict energy and forces given 3D molecular structures. Once trained, we show that the foundation models can be used in different ways to obtain geometries either explicitly or implicitly. First, it can be used to obtain low-energy 3D geometries via geometry optimization, providing relaxed 3D geometries for downstream molecular property predictions. To mitigate potential biases and enhance downstream predictions, we introduce geometry fine-tuning based on the relaxed 3D geometries. Second, the foundation models can be directly fine-tuned for property prediction when ground truth 3D geometries are available. Our results demonstrate that MLIP foundation models trained on relaxation data can provide valuable molecular geometries that benefit property predictions.
% Accurate molecular property prediction is useful in many contexts, yet many properties strongly depend on stable 3D geometries, which are computationally expensive to obtain using methods like density functional theory (DFT). To address this challenge, machine learning interatomic potential (MLIP) foundation models provide an efficient alternative for approximating 3D structures. To train such models, we curate a large-scale molecular relaxation dataset comprising 3.5 million small molecules and 400 million snapshots, each labeled with energy and force data computed at multiple levels of quantum accuracy. With the trained foundation model, 3D structures can be obtained through geometry optimization, and we further introduce geometry fine-tuning to enhance downstream property prediction using relaxed 3D structures. Additionally, the foundation model can be directly fine-tuned for 3D molecular property prediction. Our results demonstrate that the MLIP foundation model trained on large-scale relaxation data significantly improves downstream performance and paves the way for future advancements in MLIP foundation models.

\end{abstract}

