\textbf{Task.} To demonstrate the effectiveness of the MLIP foundation model for 3D molecular property prediction, we fine-tune the pre-trained foundation model on downstream tasks where the goal is to predict molecular properties given 3D molecular structures. In this setting, ground-truth geometries are used as input. We evaluate the performance on two benchmark datasets, as described below.

\textbf{Datasets.} We use Molecule3D subset created in~\cref{sec: geometry finetune} and \(\nabla^2\)DFT~\citep{khrabrov2024nabla} datasets. The details of the datasets can be found in~\cref{app: downstream dataset}. In this task, we predict the HOMO-LUMO gap, and ground truth 3D geometries are provided. Additional results on the full Molecule3D dataset are provided in~\cref{app: molecule3d full dataset}. 

% \textbf{Baselines. } We choose representative models as baselines, which include GIN-virtual~\citep{hu2021ogb}, SchNet~\citep{schutt2017schnet}, SphereNet~\citep{liu2021spherical}, ComENet~\citep{wang2022comenet}, DimeNet++~\citep{gasteiger2020fast}, TorchMD-Net~\citep{tholke2022torchmd}, Uni-Mol+~\citep{lu2023highly}, and PaiNN~\citep{schutt2021equivariant}. GIN-virtual is a powerful baseline for 2D graphs, which adds virtual node updates in the message passing. Uni-Mol+ is a state-of-the-art model that predicts both 3D geometry and ground state property during training while only needs 2D graphs during inference. For the Molecule3D, we include 2D, 3D, and hybrid method like Uni-Mol+. For $\nabla^2$DFT, we only compare 3D methods.


\textbf{Baselines.} We select a set of representative models as baselines, including GIN-virtual~\citep{hu2021ogb}, SchNet~\citep{schutt2017schnet}, SphereNet~\citep{liu2021spherical}, ComENet~\citep{wang2022comenet}, DimeNet++~\citep{gasteiger2020fast}, TorchMD-Net~\citep{tholke2022torchmd}, Uni-Mol+~\citep{lu2023highly}, and PaiNN~\citep{schutt2021equivariant}.
% GIN-virtual is a strong baseline for 2D molecular graphs that enhances message passing through a virtual node representing the global molecular context. SchNet and PaiNN are introduce in~\cref{sec: foundation model}. SphereNet incorporates directional and angular information through spherical message passing to better capture 3D molecular geometry. ComENet extends 3D message passing to ensure the full completeness of 3D information. DimeNet++ improves the accuracy and efficiency over the original DimeNet while preserving the use of angular information between edge pairs. TorchMD-Net adopts an equivariant Transformer architecture to model atomic interactions. Uni-Mol+ is a hybrid framework that learns to predict both stable 3D conformations and molecular properties during training, but requires only 2D molecular graphs at inference time.
For the Molecule3D dataset, we include 2D models, 3D models, and hybrid approaches like Uni-Mol+. For the \(\nabla^2\)DFT dataset, we focus exclusively on 3D models.

