\textbf{Analysis. } Inspired by~\citet{zaidi2022pre}, we conduct an experiment to evaluate the usefulness of pre-trained features. Specifically, we freeze the backbone and fine-tune only the prediction head, and compare it to a baseline where the backbone is randomly initialized and only the prediction head is trained. The training curves are shown in~\cref{fig:pt_random_features}. As expected, fine-tuning only the prediction head performs worse than fine-tuning the entire model, but still significantly outperforms training the head on top of random features. This indicates that pre-trained features provide a meaningful representation for the downstream task. However, to fully adapt the model and achieve optimal performance, it is necessary to fine-tune the entire network.

