\section{Computing evaluations $\alpha_i$}\label{app:evaluations Psi}
In this Appendix we compute the evaluation $\alpha_i \left( \Psi\left[E_{jj}^{\otimes k}\right]\right)$. The result is summarized in the following Lemma.
\begin{lem}\label{lem:alpha Psi}
We have the following identity:
\begin{align}\label{eq:alpha Psi}
    \alpha_i \left( \Psi\left[E_{jj}^{\otimes k}\right]\right) = 
     \begin{cases}
      \prod_{l=1}^{k}\epsilon_j\frac{ t^{\epsilon_j /2}-q^{l}t^{-\epsilon_j /2}}{1-q^{l}}  
      & i=j\\
q^{k\delta_{i>j}}\prod_{l=1}^{k}\epsilon_j\frac{ t^{\epsilon_j /2}-q^{l-1}t^{-\epsilon_j /2}}{1-q^{l}}
    & i\neq j
     \end{cases}
\end{align}
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
According to Definition \ref{def:evaluation}, in order to compute $\alpha_i$, we need to evaluate the residue of $\Psi\left[E_{jj}^{\otimes k}\right]$ at:
$$
\left\{z_1 = y, \ldots,  z_{k} = q^{k-1} y\right\}
$$
and express it in the form 
\eqref{eqn:residue (k)}, i.e.:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:residue (k) Psi}
\underset{\left\{z_1 = y, \ldots,  z_{k} = q^{k-1} y\right\}}{\normalfont{\text{Res}}}\Psi\left[E_{jj}^{\otimes k}\right] =
(t^{\frac 12} - t^{-\frac 12})^{k-1} \prod_{l=1}^{k-2} f \left(q^l \right)^{k-1-l}
 \times \check R_{1} \left(q^{-1}\right) \cdots \check R_{k-1} \left(q^{-k+1}\right) X_k^{(k)}(y)
\end{equation}
This will give us an explicit expression for $X_k^{(k)}(y)$ in which we can take the coefficient of $E_{ii}$ and derive \eqref{eq:alpha Psi}.

