Because planar geodesic orbits are a generalization of circular Keplerian orbits, we can introduce a circularity parameter to quantify the derviation of non-circular orbits, namely, 
\begin{equation}
    \gamma \equiv L\sqrt{\frac{2 f(r_0)- r_0 f'(r_0)}{r^3_0 f'(r_0)}}~.
\end{equation}
Since we have $L=\sqrt{r^3_0 f'(r_0)/(2 f(r_0)- r_0 f'(r_0))}$ for circular orbits,  $\gamma=1$ represents no deviation. Using the posterior distributions of the parameters in Fig.~\ref{non-circular orbits}, we obtain the posterior distribution of the parameter $\gamma$ shown in Fig.~\ref{post}. Our results suggest that circular orbits are preferred among planar geodesic orbits.
