\section{Introduction}

Let $\mathcal{L}(Y)$ denote the space of all linear operators $T: Y \rightarrow Y$ on a separable Banach space $Y$. For each $f \in Y$, the \textit{orbit} of $f$ under $T$ is defined by $\operatorname{Orb}(f, T) := \{T^n f : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$. A point \( f \in Y \) is called a \textit{periodic point} of \( T \) if there exists \( n \in \mathbb{N} \) such that \( T^n f = f \). The smallest such \( n \) is called the \textit{period} of \( f \).

An operator $T \in \mathcal{L}(Y)$ is said to be \textit{hypercyclic} if the orbit $Orb(f,T)$ is dense in $Y$ for some $f \in Y$. If, in addition, there is a set of periodic points of $T$  dense in $Y$, then $T$ is said to be \textit{Devaney chaotic}. On the other hand, $T\in \mathcal{L}(Y)$ is \textit{mixing} if for every pair of non-empty open sets \( U, V \subseteq Y \), there exists \( N \in \mathbb{N} \) such that $T^n(U) \cap V \neq \emptyset \quad \text{for all } n \geq N.$ These are among the most prominent notions in the theory of linear dynamics, which explores ideas at the intersection of dynamical systems and operator theory. We refer the reader to the books \cite{BayartMatheron, GrossePeris} for a comprehensive overview of the subject up to around 2010.

The concept of frequent hypercyclicity, introduced by Bayart and Grivaux in 2006 within the linear setting \cite{bayart2006frequently}, strengthens the classical notion of hypercyclicity. While hypercyclicity requires the existence of a vector whose orbit is dense in the space, frequent hypercyclicity imposes an additional condition: the orbit must return to every non-empty open set with positive lower density. This stronger requirement reflects a more regular and statistically significant recurrence behavior, establishing a deep connection with the temporal distribution of orbits and liking the theory to key ideas from ergodic theory. 

A central challenge in linear dynamics is to distinguish between chaotic and frequently hypercyclic operators. In \cite{bayart2006frequently}, Bayart and Grivaux constructed an example of a frequently hypercyclic continuous linear operator that is not chaotic. In contrast, Menet showed in \cite{menet2016chaotic} the existence of chaotic continuous linear operators that are not frequently hypercyclic. Although these notions may coincide in some cases (see, for example, \cite{BayartMatheron, DarjiPires21}), they are generally independent. 

Frequent hypercyclicity is a strong dynamical property that implies several other behaviors recently studied in the context of operators on Banach spaces, such as weak mixing, densely Li-Yorke chaos and Li-Yorke chaos (see, for instance, \cite{BayartMatheron, Bermudez2011, Bernardes2015}).

\[\xymatrix{
 &  \textbf{Frequent hypercyclic} \ar@{=>}[d]  & \textbf{Devaney chaotic} \ar@{=>}[d]  \\
\textbf{Mixing} \ar@{=>}[r] & \textbf{Weak mixing} \ar@{=>}[r] & \textbf{Hypercyclic} \ar@{=>}[d] \\
 & \textbf{Li-Yorke chaotic}  & \ar@{=>}[l] \textbf{Densely Li-Yorke chaotic} 
}\]

Hypercyclicity is closely related to the following open problem, known as the \textit{Invariant Subset Problem}: Does every operator on a Hilbert space admit a non-trivial closed invariant subset, other than the trivial ones given by $\{0\}$ and the whole space? An operator lacks non-trivial closed invariant subsets if and only if every non-zero vector is hypercyclic. In this sense, the existence of hypercyclic vectors is profoundly intertwined with the existence of invariant subsets.

Let \( H^2 (\mathbb{D})\) be the Hardy space on the open unit disk in the complex plane, which we shall refer to simply as $H^2$. We are going to focus this work on the study of the semigroup of weighted composition operators
\(\mathcal{W} = (W_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}\) on \(H^2\), where
\begin{equation*}
    W_n f(z) = (1 + z + \dots + z^{n-1}) f(z^n) = \frac{1 - z^n}{1 - z} f(z^n).
\end{equation*}
This semigroup was first introduced in \cite{Noor}. Each \(W_n\) is bounded on \(H^2\), \(W_1 = I_{H^2}\), and \(W_m W_n = W_{mn}\) for each \(m,n\in \mathbb{N}\). In~\cite{Manzur}, the relation between the semigroup \( (W_n^*)_{n \geq 2} \) and the \textit{Invariant Subspace Problem} (ISP) is explored. The ISP is closely related to the Invariant Subset Problem, but focuses on invariant subspaces rather than invariant sets. In this context, the adjoint operators \( W_n^* \) play a key role. It has been shown that for each \( n \geq 2 \), the operator \( W_n^* \) is universal in the sense of Rota: for every bounded linear operator \( T \) on a separable Hilbert space \( H \), there exists a scalar \( \alpha \neq 0 \) and an invariant subspace \( M \subset H \) such that the restriction of \( W_n^* \) to \( M \) is similar to \( \alpha T \). This universality property implies that the operator \( W_n^* \) can emulate the dynamics of any other operator, making it a powerful object of study for understanding the structure of invariant subspaces in general. These properties of the semigroups prompt the question whether their dynamics could provide further insight into their structure and behavior. So far, no results have been reported concerning the dynamics of this semigroup of operators $\mathcal{W}.$

The semigroup $\mathcal{W}$ is also related with the Riemann Hypothesis (RH), considered one of the most famous open problems in mathematics. It asserts that all nontrivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function lie on the critical line \( \Re(s) = \tfrac{1}{2} \) in the complex plane. In particular, the RH can be reformulated in terms of the behavior of certain functions within the Hardy space \( H^2 \). For each integer \( k \geq 2 \), define the function
\[h_k(z) = \frac{1}{1 - z} \log\left( \frac{1 + z + \cdots + z^{k - 1}}{k} \right),\] and let \( \mathcal{N} = \mathrm{span} \{ h_k : k \geq 2 \} \) be the linear subspace generated by these functions. The remarkable connection with the RH is captured in the following result.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite{Noor}}] 
The following statements are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The RH is true.
    \item The constant function \( 1 \) belongs to the closure of \( \mathcal{N} \) in \( H^2 \).
    \item The subspace \( \mathcal{N} \) is dense in \( H^2 \).
    \item The closure of \( \mathcal{N} \) contains a cyclic vector for the semigroup \( (W_n) \).
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

The main goal of this paper is to investigate three dynamical notions mixing, Devaney chaos and frequent hypercyclicity for the adjoint operators \( W^{\ast}_n \), with \( n \geq 2 \), and to explore how these properties relate to the Riemann Hypothesis (RH) and the existence of invariant subspaces.

This paper is organized as follows. In section \ref{Preli}, we fix the notation, present fundamental properties of the semigroup \( \mathcal{W} \) defined on \( H^2 \), and review relevant results from linear dynamics. Section \ref{Mixingchaos} is devoted to proving that the operators $W^{\ast}_n,$ for $n\geq 2,$ are mixing and, in addition, satisfy the conditions of Devaney chaos. In section \ref{Frequenthyp}, we prove that $W^{\ast}_n,$ for $n\geq 2,$ are frequently hypercyclic. Finally, in section \ref{RHIS} , we explore the relations with the RH and the existence of invariant subspaces. 

\section{Definition and background results}\label{Preli}
In this section, we establish the notation and recall preliminary concepts concerning the Hardy-Hilbert space $H^2$ and the theory of linear dynamics. We denote by $\sigma(T)$ the \emph{spectrum} of a linear operator $T$, and by $\sigma_p(T)$ its \emph{point spectrum}. Given $r > 0$, we write $\overline{B}(a, r)$ the closed ball of radius \( r \) centered at \( a \in \mathbb{C} \).

\subsection{The weighted composition semigroup  \texorpdfstring{$\mathcal{W}$}{lg}}

Let $\mathbb{D} = \{ z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| < 1 \}$ and  $\mathbb{T} = \{ z \in \mathbb{C} : |z| = 1 \}$ denote the open  unit disk and unit circle in the complex plane, respectively. The Hardy space \( H^2 := H^2(\mathbb{D}) \) is the space of all analytic functions in \( \mathbb{D} \) that admit a power series representation of the form  $f(z) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \hat{f}(n) z^n$, for which the norm
\[
\|f\|^2_{2} := \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} |\hat{f}(n)|^2 < \infty.
\]
This space is a \textit{Hilbert space} with inner product defined by:
\[
\langle f, g \rangle = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \hat{f}(n)\overline{\hat{g}(n)}.
\]
where $f,g\in H^2$. Related to this, the space $H^\infty$ consists of bounded holomorphic functions on $\mathbb{D}$:
\[ H^\infty := \left\{ f \colon \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{C}:\ f \text{ holomorphic and } \|f\|_{\infty} :=\sup_{z \in \mathbb{D}} |f(z)| < \infty \right\}.\]

\begin{definition}
Given an analytic self-map \( \varphi: \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{D} \) and an analytic function \( w: \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{C} \), the \emph{weighted composition operator} \( C_{w,\varphi} \) is defined on \( H^2 \) by
\[C_{w,\varphi}f(z) = w(z) \cdot f(\varphi(z)), \quad f \in H^2, \, z \in \mathbb{D}.\]
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
If the weight function \( w \) belongs to \( H^\infty \), then \( C_{w,\varphi} \) is a bounded operator on \( H^2 \), see \cite{Matache}.
\end{remark}

We are going to focus this work on the study of the semigroup of weighted composition operators 
\(\mathcal{W} = (W_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}\) on \(H^2\), where
\begin{equation*}
    W_n f(z) = (1 + z + \dots + z^{n-1}) f(z^n).
\end{equation*}

We first establish an adjoint formula for the elements of the semigroup \(\mathcal{W}\) which will be used throughout the rest of this work.

\begin{lemma}[{\cite[Lemma~3]{Manzur}}]\label{adjointformula}
For any \(f \in H^2\) and \(n \in \mathbb{N}\), the adjoint of \(W_n\) is given by
\[W_n^* f(z) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} B_n(k) z^k, \]
where \((f(k))_{k \in \mathbb{N}}\) are the Maclaurin coefficients of \(f\) and
\[ B_n(k) = f(nk) + f(nk + 1) + \cdots + f(nk + n - 1)\]
is the sum of the \(k\)-th block of \(n\) consecutive coefficients.
\end{lemma}

As a consequence of Lemma \ref{adjointformula}, it follows that this semigroup satisfy the property that
\begin{equation}\label{Wident}
W_n^* W_n = n I_{H^2}
\end{equation}
for all \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), and therefore that \( (W_n / \sqrt{n})_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \) is a semigroup of \emph{isometries}. Moreover, \( (W_n / \sqrt{n})_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \) define \emph{shift} semigroup. Recall that an operator $S\in \mathcal{L}(Y)$ is a shift semigroup if \( \|S^{*n} f\| \to 0 \) for all \( f \in Y \).

As a consequence of \cite[Proposition~4]{Manzur}, every \(W_n^*\) is universal in the sense of Rota and we have the following consequence. 

\begin{theorem}[{See \cite[Corollary 5]{Manzur}}]\label{EspMan} $\sigma(W_n^*) = \sigma(W_n) = \overline{B}(0, \sqrt{n}) \quad \text{for all } n \geq 2.$ Also, each \( \sigma_p(W_n) = \emptyset \), and hence \( W_n \) has no finite dimensional invariant subspaces.
\end{theorem}

The following result provides a characterization of the common eigenvectors of the semigroup \( (W_n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}} \).

\begin{proposition}[{See \cite[Proposition 22]{Calderaro2024}}]\label{ComEig}
A non-zero \( f \in H^2 \) is a common eigenvector for \( (W_n^*)_{n\in \mathbb{N}} \) if and only if there exists a multiplicative sequence \( (\lambda_n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}} \) with \( (\lambda_{n+1} - \lambda_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \in \ell^2 \) and
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:common-eigenvector}
\hat{f}(n) = (\lambda_{n+1} - \lambda_n) \hat{f}(0) \quad \forall\, n\in \mathbb{N}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, \( W_n^* f = \lambda_n f \) for all \( n\in \mathbb{N} \).
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Notions of linear dynamics}

Linear dynamics explores concepts from dynamical systems adapted for bounded linear operators on infinite-dimensional topological vector spaces. The notions of hypercyclicity, Devaney chaos, and mixing have been previously defined. We begin this section with a precise definition of frequent hypercyclicity.

\begin{definition}
Let $A$ be a subset of natural numbers. The \textit{lower density} of \( A \) is defined by
\[\underline{\mathrm{dens}}(A) := \liminf_{N \to \infty} \frac{\mathrm{card}(A \cap [1, N])}{N}.\]
\end{definition}
Let us define 
\[\mathrm{N}(x, V) := \{ n \in \mathbb{N} \, ; \, T^n(x) \in V \}\]

\begin{definition}
Let \( T \in \mathcal{L}(Y) \). The operator \( T \) is said to be \textit{frequently hypercyclic} if there exists some vector \( y \in Y \) such that \( N(y, V) \) has positive lower density for every non-empty open set \( V \subset Y \). Such a vector \( x \) is said to be frequently hypercyclic for \( T \), and the set of all frequently hypercyclic vectors for \( T \) is denoted by \( \mathrm{FHC}(T) \).
\end{definition}

As consequence of \cite[Theorem~9.8]{GrossePeris} every frequently hypercyclic operator is hypercyclic. 

\begin{definition}
An operator $T\in\mathcal{L}(Y)$ is \textit{Li-Yorke chaotic} if it has an uncountable \textit{scrambled set} $S$, i.e., for all $x, y \in S$ with $x\neq y$, we have that $$\displaystyle\liminf_{n\to \infty}\|T^{n}x-T^{n}y\|=0\ and 
 \ \displaystyle\limsup_{n\to \infty}\|T^{n}x-T^{n}y\|=\infty.$$
If the set \( S \) can be chosen to be dense in \( Y \), then \( f \) is \textit{densely Li-Yorke chaotic}.  \end{definition}

It is well known that every hypercyclic operator is Li-Yorke chaotic (see \cite[Remark~22]{Bernardes2015}). In particular, every frequently hypercyclic operator is Li-Yorke chaotic. 

An important consequence of the characterization theorem for Li-Yorke chaos (\cite[Theorem~5]{Bermudez2011}) is the following corollary.

\begin{corollary}[{See \cite[Corollary 6]{Bermudez2011}}]\label{LiNec}
Let \( T\in \mathcal{L}(Y) \) be a Li-Yorke chaotic operator. The following assertions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \( \sigma(T) \cap \mathbb{T} \neq \emptyset. \)
    \item \( T \) is not normal.
    \item \( T \) is not compact.
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}

 \begin{proposition}[{See \cite[Proposition 11]{Bernardes2015}}]
If \( T \in \mathcal{L}(Y) \) and \( T^* \) has an eigenvalue \( \lambda \) with \( |\lambda| \geq 1 \), then \( T \) is not densely Li-Yorke chaotic.
\end{proposition}

As a consequence of the Proposition above we can see that $W_k$, for each $k\geq1$, \textit{is not densely Li-Yorke chaotic} since $W_k^*1=1$, $\forall k\geq1$.  The natural question that arises is the following: Is each operator $W_k$, $k\geq2$, Li–Yorke chaotic? Note first that the three necessary conditions stated in Corollary~\ref{LiNec} are satisfied:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item By Theorem \ref{EspMan}, \( \sigma(W_k) \cap \mathbb{T} \neq \emptyset \).
    \item $W_k$ is not normal since 
    \begin{align*}
        W_kW_k^*1=1+z+\dots+z^{k-1}\neq k=W_k^*W_k1,\ \ \forall k\geq2.
    \end{align*}
    \item  $W_k$ is not a compact operator since it has no eigenvalues and $\sigma(W_k)$ is non-empty (see \cite[Theorem 8.4-4]{Kreyszig}).
\end{enumerate}

These observations suggest that each operator $W_k$, $k\geq2$, is Li–Yorke chaotic. However, in the next section we shall demonstrate that this assertion is false.

\section{A semigroup of mixing and Devaney chaotic operators}\label{Mixingchaos}

In this section we shall prove that the elements of the semigroup $(W_k^*)_{k\geq2}$ are mixing and Devaney chaotic operators. In order to do that, we are going to use the following result:

\begin{theorem}[Godefroy--Shapiro Criterion {\cite[Theorem 3.1]{GrossePeris}}] \label{GSC}
Let \( T \in \mathcal{L}(Y) \). Suppose that
\[\bigcup_{|\lambda| < 1} \ker(T - \lambda I) \quad \text{and} \quad \bigcup_{|\lambda| > 1} \ker(T - \lambda I) \]
both span a dense subspace of  \( Y\). Then \( T \) is mixing, and in particular hypercyclic.\vspace{2mm}

If, moreover, \( Y \) is a complex space and also the subspace
\[Z_0 := \mathrm{span} \{ x \in Y \; ; \; Tx = e^{\alpha \pi i} x \text{ for some } \alpha \in \mathbb{Q} \}\]
is dense in \( Y \), then \( T \) is Devaney chaotic.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}\label{PerChar}
Let us denote \(\mathrm{Per}(T) \) the set of periodic points of $T$. In  {\cite[Proposition 2.33]{GrossePeris}} it is shown that \[ \mathrm{Per}(T)= \mathrm{span} \{ x \in X \; ; \; Tx = e^{\alpha \pi i} x \text{ for some } \alpha \in \mathbb{Q} \}.\]
\end{remark}

\subsection{A mixing semigroup}   Let us define the family of functions $(f_k)_{k\in \mathbb{N}}$ in $H^2$ given by
\begin{align*}
    f_k(z)=1+\sum_{n=1}^\infty \left( \dfrac{1}{(n+1)^k}- \dfrac{1}{n^k}\right)z^n=(1-z)\sum_{n=0}^\infty \dfrac{1}{(n+1)^k}z^n. 
\end{align*}
 By Proposition \ref{ComEig}, $(f_k)_{k\in \mathbb{N}}$ are common eigenvector for the semigroup $(W_n^*)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$; that is,
 \begin{align*}
   W_n^* f_k= \dfrac{1}{n^k}f_k,\ \ \forall k\geq1, \ \ \forall n\geq1.  
 \end{align*}
Let us define 
\begin{align*}
     g_k(z)=\sum_{n=0}^\infty \dfrac{1}{(n+1)^k}z^n.
\end{align*}

\begin{lemma}
 $(g_k)_{k\in \mathbb{N}}$ span a dense subspace of $H^2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $g\in \{g_k\}^\perp_{k\in \mathbb{N}}$. Then
\begin{align*}
 \sum_{n=0}^\infty \dfrac{\hat{g}(n)}{(n+1)^k}=0\Longleftrightarrow  \sum_{n=1}^\infty \dfrac{\hat{g}(n)}{(n+1)^k}=-\hat{g}(0),\ \ \forall k\geq1.
\end{align*}
It follows that
\begin{align*}
|\hat{g}(0)|&=\left|\left\langle \sum_{n=1}^\infty \hat{g}(n)z^n,\sum_{n=1}^\infty \dfrac{z^n}{(n+1)^{k+l}}\right\rangle\right|\\&\leq \left \| \sum_{n=1}^\infty \hat{g}(n)z^n\right \| \left(\sum_{n=1}^\infty \dfrac{1}{(n+1)^{2(k+l)}}\right)^{1/2} \\&\leq \dfrac{1}{2^l}\left \| \sum_{n=1}^\infty \hat{g}(n)z^n\right \|  \left \|  \sum_{n=1}^\infty \dfrac{z^n}{(n+1)^{k}} \right \|,\ \ \forall k,l\geq 1.  
\end{align*}
Fixing \( k \) and letting \( l \to \infty \), it follows that $\hat{g}(0)=0$. By induction, suppose that $\hat{g}(1)=\dots=\hat{g}(s-1)=0$. Now, using the same arguments as above, we get
\begin{align*}
\left|\dfrac{\hat{g}(s)}{(s+1)^{k+l}}\right|&=\left|\left\langle \sum_{n=s+1}^\infty \hat{g}(n)z^n,\sum_{n=s+1}^\infty \dfrac{z^n}{(n+1)^{k+l}}\right\rangle\right|\\&\leq \dfrac{1}{(s+2)^l}\left \| \sum_{n=s+1}^\infty \hat{g}(n)z^n\right \|  \left \|  \sum_{n=s+1}^\infty \dfrac{z^n}{(n+1)^{k}} \right \|,\ \ \forall k,l\geq 1. \end{align*}
Then,
\begin{align*}
\left|\dfrac{\hat{g}(s)}{(s+1)^{k}}\right|\leq \left(\dfrac{s+1}{s+2}\right)^l\left \| \sum_{n=s+1}^\infty \hat{g}(n)z^n\right \|  \left \|  \sum_{n=s+1}^\infty \dfrac{z^n}{(n+1)^{k}} \right \|,\ \ \forall k,l\geq 1.  
\end{align*}
Fixing \( k \) and letting \( l \to \infty \), the result follows.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{ftotal}
  $(f_k)_{k\in \mathbb{N}}$ span a dense subspace of $H^2$.   
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
    It follows from the fact that $I-S$ has a dense range, where $(Sf)(z)=zf(z)$ is the multiplication operator by $z$. 
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{dense1}
  $\displaystyle\bigcup_{|\lambda| < 1} \ker(W_n^* - \lambda I)$ span a dense subspace of $H^2$ for all $n\geq2$.  
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Since \( (f_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}} \subset \displaystyle\bigcup_{|\lambda| < 1} \ker(W_n^* - \lambda I) \), the result follows from Corollary~\ref{ftotal}.  
\end{proof}

Now, we will explore the eigenvalues whose modulus is greather than 1.  Let \( H^2(\mathbb{C}_{1/2}) \) be the \textit{Hardy space of the open right half-plane} \( \mathbb{C}_{1/2} = \{ \Re s > 1/2 \} \). This is a Hilbert space of analytic functions on \( \mathbb{C}_{1/2} \) for which the norm is given by
\[ \|f\|_2^2 = \sup_{0 < x < \infty} \frac{1}{\pi} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} |f(x + iy)|^2 \, dy. \]

In \cite{Ghosh2024} it was introduced a linear functional \( \Lambda^{(s)}:= E_s \circ \Lambda : H^2\to \mathbb{C} \), where $E_s: H^2(\mathbb{C}_{1/2}) \to \mathbb{C}$ is the evaluation functional at \( s \in \mathbb{C} \setminus \{0\} \) and $\Lambda: H^2 \to H^2(\mathbb{C}_{1/2})$ is the result of a composition of certain isometries. \( \Lambda^{(s)}\)  can be defined by assigning
\[
\Lambda^{(s)}(1) = -\frac{1}{s}, \quad \Lambda^{(s)}(z^k) = -\frac{1}{s} \left( (k+1)^{1-s} - k^{1-s} \right) \quad (k \in \mathbb{N}).
\]

In particular \( \Lambda^{(s)} \) is bounded on \( H^2 \)  if \( \Re s > 1/2 \). So there exist functions \( \kappa_s \in H^2 \) such that \( \Lambda^{(s)}(f) = \langle f, \kappa_s \rangle \). The function \( \kappa_s \) it is called the zeta kernel at \( s \) and

\begin{equation}
\kappa_s(z) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \varphi_k(\bar{s}) z^k \quad \text{where} \quad \varphi_k(s) := \Lambda^{(s)}(z^k).
\end{equation}

In  \cite{Calderaro2024} it was shown that the set $\mathcal{K}=\{ \kappa_s: \Re s > 1/2 \}$ is a family of common eigenvectors for $(W_k^*)_{k\in \mathbb{N}}$ with  \[W_n^* \kappa_s = n^{1 - \overline{s}} \kappa_s \quad \forall\, n\in \mathbb{N}.\]
In particular, $|n^{1 - \overline{s}}|=n^{1 -\Re s} >1$ for $1/2<\Re s < 1$.

\begin{lemma}\label{ktotal}
$\{ \kappa_s:  1/2<\Re s < 1 \}$ span a dense subspace in $H^2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
 Let $f\in\{ \kappa_s:  1/2<\Re s < 1 \}^{\perp}$. Then
\begin{align*}
    \Lambda(f)(s)=\Lambda^{(s)}(f)=\langle f,\kappa_s\rangle=0,\ \ \forall \  1/2<\Re s < 1.
\end{align*}
Since \( \Lambda(f) \) is an analytic function that vanishes on a non-empty open set, it must be identically zero; that is, \( \Lambda(f) = 0 \). Moreover, since \( \Lambda \) is a composition of isometries, it is in particular injective (see \cite{Ghosh2024}). Hence, \( f = 0 \), and the assertion follows.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{dense2}
  $\displaystyle\bigcup_{|\lambda| > 1} \ker(W_n^* - \lambda I)$ span a dense subspace of $H^2$ for all $n\geq2$.  
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Since \( \{ \kappa_s : 1/2 < \Re s < 1 \} \subset \displaystyle\bigcup_{|\lambda| > 1} \ker(W_n^* - \lambda I) \), the result follows from Lemma~\ref{ktotal}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{mixingthm}
$W_n^*$ is mixing and, in particular, hypercyclic operator for all $n\geq2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
It is a consequence of Proposition \ref{dense1}, Proposition \ref{dense2} and the Godefroy-Shapiro Criterion.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
 $W_n^*$ is a densely Li-Yorke chaotic operator for all $n\geq2$   
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Hypercyclicity implies densely Li-Yorke chaos (see \cite{Bernardes2015}).
\end{proof}

In the previous section, we raised the question of whether \( W_k \)  is Li–Yorke chaotic. We now answer this question.

%In the previous section, we raised the question of whether  is Li-Yorke chaotic. We now proceed to answer this question.

\begin{proposition}
The family \( (W_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \) forms a semigroup of operators that are not Li-Yorke chaotic.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Recall that an operator \(T \in \mathcal{L}(Y)\) is \textit{uniformly positively expansive} if there exists $n \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $$z \in S_Y:=\{y\in Y: \|y\|=1\} \Longrightarrow \ \|T^n z\| \geq 2. $$ Since \( W_k / \sqrt{k} \) is an isometry, it follows that for any \( f \in S_{H^2} \), %(the unit sphere of \( H^2 \)), 
\[\| W_k^n f \| = \| W_{k^n} f \| = \sqrt{k}^n.\]
Thus, for \( n \geq 2 \) and \( k \geq 2 \), we obtain that \( W_n \) is uniformly positively expansive. From \cite[Theorem C]{Bernardes2018}, \( W_k \), with \( k \geq 2 \), can not be Li-Yorke chaotic. Moreover, when \( k = 1 \), the operator \( W_1=I_{H^2}\), and it is clear that this operator is not chaotic.
\end{proof}

\subsection{A Devaney chaotic semigroup} To conclude this section, we will show that the operators \( W_n^* \) are not only hypercyclic but also Devaney chaotic for all \( n \geq 2 \). \vspace{2mm}

First, observe that
\[
\{ \kappa_s : \Re(s) = 1 \} \subset \bigcup_{|\lambda| = 1} \ker(W_n^* - \lambda I),
\]
since for \( s = 1 + it \), with \( t \in \mathbb{R} \), we have \( W_n^* \kappa_s = n^{1 - \overline{s}} \kappa_s = n^{it} \kappa_s \), and \( |n^{it}| = 1 \). Now, noting that \( n^{it} = e^{it \log n} \), we define
\[
t_{n,\alpha} := \frac{\pi \alpha}{\log n}, \quad \alpha \in \mathbb{Q},
\]
so that, by Remark~\ref{PerChar}, we obtain
\[
\kappa_{s_{n,\alpha}} \in \mathrm{Per}(W_n^*) \quad \text{for all } n \geq 2,
\]
where \( s_{n,\alpha} = 1 + i t_{n,\alpha} \). Since \( \mathbb{Q} \) is dense in \( \mathbb{R} \), it follows that \( \{ t_{n,\alpha} \}_{\alpha \in \mathbb{Q}} \) is dense in \( \mathbb{R} \), and therefore \( \{ s_{n,\alpha} \}_{\alpha \in \mathbb{Q}} \) is dense in the vertical line \( \{ \Re(s) = 1 \} \), for each $n\geq2$.

\begin{lemma}\label{k1total}
$\{ \kappa_{s_{n,\alpha}}\}_{\alpha\in\mathbb{Q}}$ span a dense subspace in $H^2$, for all $n\geq2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let \( f \in \{ \kappa_{s_{n,\alpha}} \}_{\alpha \in \mathbb{Q}}^\perp \). Then
\[
\Lambda(f)(s_{n,\alpha}) = \Lambda^{(s_{n,\alpha})}(f) = \langle f, \kappa_{s_{n,\alpha}} \rangle = 0.
\]
Since the set \( \{ s_{n,\alpha} \}_{\alpha \in \mathbb{Q}} \) is dense in the vertical line \( \{ \Re(s) = 1 \} \), the zero set of \( \Lambda(f) \) has an accumulation point (in fact, every point on the line is an accumulation point). As \( \Lambda(f) \) is analytic, it must vanish identically; that is, \( \Lambda(f) = 0 \). Therefore, \( f = 0 \), and the result follows.
\end{proof}
% \begin{proposition}\label{ktotal2}
% $\mathrm{Per}(W_n^*)$ is a dense subspace of $H^2$, for all $n\geq2$.
% \end{proposition}
% \begin{proof}
% Since $\{ \kappa_{s_{n,\alpha}}\}_{\alpha\in\mathbb{Q}}\subset \mathrm{Per}(W_n^*) $, the proposition follows from the previous Lemma.    
% \end{proof}
\begin{theorem}
    $W_n^{\ast}$ is a Devaney chaotic operator for all $n\geq 2.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since \( \{ \kappa_{s_{n,\alpha}} \}_{\alpha \in \mathbb{Q}} \subset \mathrm{Per}(W_n^*) \), it follows that the set of periodic points \( \mathrm{Per}(W_n^*) \) is dense in \( H^2 \). Moreover, Theorem~\ref{mixingthm} establishes that \( W_n^* \) is hypercyclic; therefore, by the Godefroy–Shapiro Criterion ensures that \( W_n^* \) is Devaney chaotic.
\end{proof}


\section{A semigroup of frequently hypercyclic operators}\label{Frequenthyp}

This section is devoted to proving that the semigroup \( (W_n^{\ast})_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \) consists of frequently hypercyclic operators. To this end, we begin by presenting some preliminary results.

\begin{definition}
We say that a Banach space \( Y \) is of \textit{type \( p \)} for some \( 1 \leq p \leq 2 \) if there exists a constant \( C \geq 1 \) such that
\[
\left\| \sum_{i=1}^n \varepsilon_i x_i \right\|_{L^2(Y)} \leq C \left( \sum_{i=1}^n \|x_i\|^p \right)^{1/p}
\]
for every \( x_1, \ldots, x_n \in Y \), where \( (\varepsilon_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}} \) is a Bernoulli sequence.
\end{definition}

In \cite{LiQueffelec2017} it is shown that every Hilbert space is type 2.

\begin{definition}
A complex measure \( \sigma \) on \( \mathbb{T} \) is said to be \textit{continuous} if it has no discrete part, i.e. \( \sigma(\{\lambda\}) = 0 \) for every \( \lambda \in \mathbb{T} \). 
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
Let \( T \in \mathcal{L}(Y) \). Given a probability measure \( \sigma \) on \( \mathbb{T} \), we say that \( T \in \mathcal{L}(Y) \) has a \textit{\( \sigma \)-spanning set of \( \mathbb{T} \)-eigenvectors} if for every \( \sigma \)-measurable subset \( A \subset \mathbb{T} \) with \( \sigma(A) = 1 \), the eigenspaces \( \ker(T - \lambda I) \), \( \lambda \in A \), span a dense subspace of \( Y \). If \( T \) has a \( \sigma \)-spanning set of \( \mathbb{T} \)-eigenvectors for some continuous measure \( \sigma \), then we say that \( T \) has a \textit{perfectly spanning set of \( \mathbb{T} \)-eigenvectors}.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[{See \cite[Corollary 6.24]{BayartMatheron}}]\label{FreqEig}
Let \( Y \) be a complex separable infinite-dimensional Banach space, and let \( T \in \mathcal{L}(Y) \). If \( Y \) is of type 2 and \( T \) admits a perfectly spanning set of \( \mathbb{T} \)-eigenvectors, then \( T \) is frequently hypercyclic.
\end{proposition}

Let us denote $m$ the normalized Lebesgue measure on $\mathbb{T}$, which is a probability measure on this space.

\begin{lemma}
Let \( A \subset \mathbb{T} \) be a \( m \)-measurable subset such that \( m(A) = 1 \). Then \( A \) has no isolated points.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Suppose that \( p\) is an isolated point in \( A \). Then there exists an open set \( V_p \subset \mathbb{T} \) such that $V_p \cap A = \{p\}$. We also have that
\[
m(\mathbb{T}) = m(\mathbb{T} \setminus A) + m(A).
\]
As \( m(A) = 1 \), it follows that \( m(\mathbb{T} \setminus A) = 0 \), which is a contradiction because \( \mathbb{T} \setminus A \) contains the non-empty open set \( V_p \setminus \{p\} \), which must have positive measure.
\end{proof}

Under the conditions of the previous lemma, every point in \( A \) is an accumulation point. In particular, $A$ cannot be a  finite set. \vspace{2mm}

Let us define the map, for each $n\geq2,$
\[
\varphi_n : \left(0, \frac{2\pi}{\log n}\right) \to \mathbb{T} \setminus \{1\}, \quad \varphi_n(t) = e^{it \log n}.
\]
Each \( \varphi_n \) is a homeomorphism, as it arises from the classical homeomorphism
\[
\varphi : (0, 2\pi) \to \mathbb{T} \setminus \{1\}, \quad \varphi(t) = e^{it},
\]
%via the change of variable \( t \mapsto t \log n \).
\begin{lemma}
    Let \( A \subset \mathbb{T} \) be a \( m \)-measurable subset such that \( m(A) = 1 \). Then $\{ \kappa_{1+it}\}_{t\in \varphi_n^{-1}(A)}$ span a dense subspace in $H^2$, for all $n\geq2$.  
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
 Let \( f \in \{ \kappa_{1+it}\}_{t\in \varphi_n^{-1}(A)}^\perp \). Then
\[
\Lambda(f)(1+it) = \Lambda^{(1+it)}(f) = \langle f, \kappa_{1+it} \rangle = 0,\ \ \forall t\in \varphi_n^{-1}(A).
\]
Since \( A \) cannot be a finite set, let us take \( a \in A \setminus \{1\} \). As \( a \) is an accumulation point of \( A \), it follows that \( t_0 = \varphi_n^{-1}(a)\) is an accumulation point of \( \varphi_n^{-1}(A) \) by the continuity. Therefore, the zero set of \( \Lambda(f) \) has an accumulation point. Since \( \Lambda(f) \) is analytic, it must vanish identically; that is, \( \Lambda(f) = 0 \). Hence, \( f = 0 \), and the result follows.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{KerMeasure}
    Let \( A \subset \mathbb{T} \) be a \( m \)-measurable subset such that \( m(A) = 1 \). Then  \(\displaystyle\bigcup_{\lambda\in A} \ker(W_n^* - \lambda I) \) span a dense subspace of $H^2$, for all $n\geq2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    Note that if \( t \in \varphi_n^{-1}(A) \), then
    \[
    W_n^* \kappa_{1+it} = n^{it} \kappa_{1+it} = e^{it\log n} \kappa_{1+it}.
    \]
    Since \( \varphi_n(t) = e^{it\log n} \in A \), it follows that
    \[
    \left\{ \kappa_{1+it} \right\}_{t \in \varphi_n^{-1}(A)} \subset \bigcup_{\lambda \in A} \ker(W_n^* - \lambda I),
    \]
    and the assertion follows from the previous lemma.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
$W_n^{\ast}$ is a frequently hypercyclic operator for all $n\geq 2.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since \( m \) is a continuous measure, Proposition \ref{KerMeasure} implies that \( W_n^{\ast} \) has a perfectly spanning set of \( \mathbb{T}\)-eigenvectors. Then, by Proposition \ref{FreqEig}, the assertion follows.
\end{proof}

\section{Riemann hypothesis and invariant subspaces related to the semigroup \texorpdfstring{$(W_k^*)_{k\geq2}$}{lg}}\label{RHIS}

In \cite{Calderaro2024} it was shown that $W_n$ does not have finite-dimensional invariant subspaces for all $n\geq2$. We provide a new proof for this result.

\begin{theorem}
Let $S$ be a non-trivial closed \( W_n \)-invariant subspace for some $n\geq2$. Then $S$ is infinite-dimensional.    
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Note that 
\begin{align*}
    W_n S\subset S\Longleftrightarrow W_n^* S^\perp\subset S^\perp.
\end{align*}
Since \( W_n^* \) is hypercyclic, $S=S^{\perp\perp}$ is infinite-dimensional, see \cite[Page~15]{BayartMatheron}.
\end{proof}

We now consider the space \( \mathcal{N} = \mathrm{span}(h_k)_{k \geq 2} \), which is connected to the Riemann Hypothesis in the following sense:

\begin{theorem}[{See \cite[Theorem 8]{Noor}}]
    RH holds if and only if $\mathcal{N}$ is dense in $H^2$.
\end{theorem}

We now introduce a new equivalent formulation for the RH involving hypercyclic vectors for the opertors $W_k^*$.

\begin{theorem}
  Let \( k \geq 2 \). Then the RH holds if and only if there exists a hypercyclic vector \( f \) for \( W_k^* \) and \( N \in \mathbb{N} \) such that
\[ W_k^{*n} f \in \overline{\mathcal{N}} \quad \text{for all } n \geq N. \]

\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If the RH is true, then \( \overline{\mathcal{N}} = H^2 \). Since \( W_k^* \) is a hypercyclic operator, there exists a hypercyclic vector \( f \), and the assertion follows. Conversely, suppose that there exist a hypercyclic vector \( f \) for \( W_k^* \) and an integer \( N \in \mathbb{N} \) such that
\[
W_k^{*n} f \in \overline{\mathcal{N}} \quad \text{for all } n \geq N.
\]
As the sequence \( (W_k^{*n} f)_{n \geq 0} \) is dense in \( H^2 \), the subsequence \( (W_k^{*n} f)_{n \geq N} \) is also dense in \( H^2 \). Since this subsequence is entirely contained in \( \overline{\mathcal{N}} \), it follows that \( \overline{\mathcal{N}} = H^2 \), and thus the RH holds.
\end{proof}

The density of \( \mathcal{N} \) is equivalent to the condition \( \mathcal{N}^\perp = \{0\} \). There has been interest in studying which subspaces \( X \) satisfy \( \mathcal{N}^\perp \cap X = \{0\} \); see, for instance, \cite{Calderaro2024, Noor}. We will now show that the set of hypercyclic vectors for $W_k^*$ has no elements in common with \( \mathcal{N}^\perp \) and, in addition, this set is dense in $H^2$. 

Let $HC(W^{\ast}_n)$ be the set of hypercyclic vectors of $W^{\ast}_n$. Recall that a \( G_\delta \) set is a subset of a topological space that can be expressed as a countable intersection of open sets.

\begin{proposition}
 \( HC(W_n^*) \) is a dense \( G_\delta \) set in $H^2$ for all $n\geq2$.   
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
This follows from Birkhoff's Transitivity Theorem; see \cite[Theorem 2.19]{GrossePeris}.
\end{proof}

As a consequence of this Proposition, we get the following Corollary.

\begin{corollary}\label{RHHC}
    RH holds if and only if  \( HC(W_n^*) \subset \overline{\mathcal{N}}\) for some $n\geq2$.
\end{corollary}

As shown in Corollary~\ref{RHHC}, if the RH is true, then \( HC(W_n^*) \subset \overline{\mathcal{N}} \) for some \( n \geq 2 \). In that case, since \( \overline{\mathcal{N}} \cap \mathcal{N}^\perp = \{0\} \), it follows that \( HC(W_n^*) \cap \mathcal{N}^\perp = \varnothing \). In what follows, we will prove that this conclusion holds unconditionally.

% \begin{proposition}\label{hypden}
% If \( T \in \mathcal{L}(Y) \) is hypercyclic, and \( L \subset Y \) is a closed \( T \)-invariant set that contains a hypercyclic vector, then \( L = Y \).
% \end{proposition}
% \begin{proof}
%     Let \( x \in L \) be a hypercyclic vector for \( T \). Since \( L \) is invariant under \( T \), it follows that \( T^n x \in L \) for every \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), and hence \( \operatorname{Orb}(x, T) \subset L \). Because the orbit is dense in \( Y \) and is contained in \( L \) which is closed, we conclude that \( L = Y\).
% \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{HCperp}
Let \( T  \in \mathcal{L}(Y) \) be a  hypercyclic operator, $V$ a set (subspace) of hypercyclic vectors for $T$ (except for zero), and let \( M \subset Y\) be a closed, proper, \( T \)-invariant subspace. Then
\[ M \cap V = \varnothing\ \ \ \ (\mathrm{or}\ \  M \cap V = \{0\}).\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists a non-zero element \( f \in M \cap V \). Since \( M \) is a closed and \( W_n^* \)-invariant subspace, it follows that \(\operatorname{Orb}(f,T)\) is contained in \( M \). However, as \( f \) is hypercyclic, this implies that \( M = Y \), contradicting the assumption that \( M \) is a proper set (subspace).
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{Nperp}
    For all \( n \geq 2 \), we have \( \mathcal{N}^\perp \cap HC(W_n^*) = \varnothing \).
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
If \( \mathcal{N}^\perp \) is not proper, then \( \mathcal{N}^\perp = \{0\} \), and the statement follows immediately. On the other hand, if \( \mathcal{N}^\perp \) is proper, as it is also \( W_n^* \)-invariant closed subspace (see \cite{Noor}), for Theorem \ref{HCperp} the conclusion holds.
\end{proof}

Define \( \mathcal{M} := \mathrm{span} \{ h_k - h_\ell : k, \ell \geq 2 \} \). The subspace \( \mathcal{M} \) is proper and $W_n$-invariant, satisfying \( \mathcal{M} \subset \mathcal{N} \) and $\dim \mathcal{M}^\perp \geq 1$. It was shown in \cite{Manzur} that
\[
    \mathrm{RH} \Longleftrightarrow \dim \mathcal{M}^\perp = 1.
\]
As in Corollary~\ref{Nperp}, we can establish the following:
\begin{corollary}
    For all \( n \geq 2 \), we have \( \mathcal{M}^\perp \cap HC(W_n^*) = \varnothing \).
\end{corollary}

The previous result focused on sets of hypercyclic vectors. We now proceed to examine results involving subspaces formed by hypercyclic vectors. In the Herrero--Bourdon Theorem \cite[Theorem 2.55]{GrossePeris}, the invariant subspace consisting of hypercyclic vectors (except for zero) that is chosen to be \textit{dense} is precisely the space
\[
Z_x(T) := \{ p(T)x \; ; \; p \text{ is a polynomial} \} = \mathrm{span} \, \operatorname{Orb}(x, T)
\]
where $x$ is any hypercyclic vector for $T$.

The next result follows as Corollary~\ref{Nperp}; hence, we omit the proof.

\begin{corollary}
Let \( f \) be a hypercyclic vector for \( W_n^* \). Then, for all \( n \geq 2 \), we have
\[
\mathcal{N}^\perp \cap Z_f(W_n^*) = \{0\}.
\]

\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
Let \( f \) be a hypercyclic vector for \( W_n^* \). Then, for all \( n \geq 2 \), we have
\[\mathcal{M}^\perp \cap Z_f(W_n^*) = \{0\}.\]
\end{corollary}

