        0 & \lesssim & \frac{n_e}{n}\lesssim \frac{1}{27},\nm\\              %%\lessapprox
        \frac{1}{27} & \lesssim & \frac{n_{ub}}{n}\lesssim \frac{1}{9},\nm\\
        \frac{1}{9} & \lesssim & \frac{n_{ur}}{n}\lesssim \frac{1}{6},\nm\\
        \frac{1}{6} & \lesssim & \frac{n_{dr}}{n}\lesssim \frac{2}{9},\nm\\
        \frac{2}{9} & \lesssim & \frac{n_{db}}{n}\lesssim \frac{8}{27}.
%    \end{aligned}
\ee
From these relations, we can obtain that $0.11\lesssim(n_{dr}-n_{ub})/n\lesssim 0.13$ which hardly changes with the chemical potential. In this model, including the second term of Eq.~(\ref{eq23}) results in a slight increase in the electron fraction from $1/27\approx0.037$ to $0.045$ when $\mu=500$~MeV.

