\begin{comment}
Consider an ensemble of $N_u$ users, each user $i$ with activity $\eta_i$, defined as the number of comments s/he posted. \NoteR{See below.}
We sample the activity $\eta$ of users from a probability distribution $P \left( \eta \right)$. 
The probability $p_i$ that a comment is posted by a user with activity $\eta_i$ is thus proportional to $\eta_i$, 
\begin{equation}
    p_i = \frac{\eta_i}{\sum_{j=1}^{N_u} \eta_j }.
\label{eq:assignation}
\end{equation}
\NoteR{With this definition we do not need to impose that $\eta$ is proportional to number of comments posted, it comes as a consequence. The equality is used as a operational definition in the real data.}
\end{comment}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
Comments in the thread are created by a population of $U$ users, with each user producing a total number of comments equal to $\eta$, obeying the distribution $P(\eta)$. 
If a comment draws the attention of the post's creator, it can be rewarded with a positive acknowledgment. We refer to the authors of rewarded comments as \emph{receivers}, while we refer to the probability that users are rewarded by their \emph{fitness} $\rho$. We assume that the fitness $\rho$ is solely a function of the user's activity $\eta$: The more comments a user posts, the more likely the user is rewarded. For simplicity, we consider $\rho \sim \eta^\omega$, with $\omega > 0$. 
%so that users are completely characterized by the distribution $P \left( \eta \right)$ and the exponent $\omega$. 
The probability that 
a randomly selected comment, written by a user with activity $\eta$, is rewarded is
%a user with activity $\eta$ is the author of a randomly selected comment that is rewarded is
\begin{equation}
p_r(\eta) = \frac{\eta^{\omega + 1}}{U \, n_0 \, \av{\eta^{\omega + 1}}} 
\; ,
\label{eq:prob1}
\end{equation}
where $n_0$ is the rate of rewarded comments and $\av{\ldots}$ denotes the mean value, see End Matter for details. 
%As shown in the Supplementary Material (SM) , the above expression is obtained by simply noticing that the probability that a randomly selected comment is generated by a user with activity $\eta$ is proportional to $\eta P(\eta)$. 
Importantly, Eq.~\eqref{eq:prob1} is valid for a single comment. In case of $n(t)$ multiple comments posted on day $t$, the probability that 
at least one of the comments written by a user with activity $\eta$ is rewarded 
%a user with activity $\eta$ writes at least one rewarded comment 
is given by $1 - \left[1 - p_r(\eta)\right]^{n(t)}$.
