Next, we model the attention dynamics of the original post's creator. 
The post will attract a certain number of comments per day $n(t)$, and if a comment draws the creator's attention, s/he can reward the comment's author with a positive acknowledgment. 
We refer to the users rewarded as \emph{receivers}, while we indicate the probability that a user $i$ is rewarded by their \emph{fitness} $\rho_i$. 
We assume that the fitness $\rho$ is solely a function of the activity $\eta$: The more comments a user posts, the more likely they are rewarded. 
For simplicity, we will consider $\rho \sim \eta^\omega$, with $\omega > 0$. 
Therefore, users are completely characterized by the distribution $P \left( \eta \right)$ and the exponent $\omega$.

We are interested in modeling the probability $r_i(t)$ that a comment posted on day $t$ by a user with fitness $\rho_i$ is rewarded. 
Such a probability will be proportional to the fitness of user $i$ and to the number of comments posted on day $t$ (which in turn will be proportional to the comments posted by user $i$ via Eq.~\eqref{eq:assignation}),
%Therefore, the dynamics of the model will be governed by the probability $r_i(t)$ that a comment posted on day $t$ by a user with fitness $\rho_i$ is rewarded, with
