For example, if a comment is posted on day $t = 3$ and is rewarded by the post's creator, the reward day is $t^* = 3$. 
\NoteR{We assume that all posts are rewarded (or not) the day of their creation.}
Therefore, we characterize the probability distribution that a comment is rewarded at a certain day $t^*$, $P \left( t^* \right)$. 
\NoteR{I do not think $P(t^*)$ measures the evolution over time of attention as claimed above.}
To this aim, we will use as the model's inputs the number of comments $n(t)$ of the posts throughout the days, the distributions $P \left( N \right)$ and $P \left( T \right)$ describing the total number of comments and duration of the posts, respectively, and the distribution $P \left( \eta \right)$ indicating the activity of users. 
We will first provide an analytical solution for $P \left( t^* \right)$ with the inputs given by mathematical functions, and next we will validate our model with the inputs given by empirical functions.
\end{comment}

