\emph{Discussion}. 
In this work, we addressed a key gap in the study of attention dynamics by shifting the focus from collective behavior to an individual level. 
While much of the previous research has examined how information spreads, trends emerge, or memes compete at large scale, we focus on a microscopic perspective: 
How a single user allocates their attention in the face of many competing stimuli. 
%This individual-centric approach is especially relevant in today's digital landscape, where users are routinely inundated with replies, notifications, messages, and tasks---each demanding a fragment of their finite attention.

Our main contribution is a minimal, analytically tractable model that captures how individual attention decays over time, as a function of the total duration of interactions, rather than their number or the activity levels of interacting users. 
%This is due to the fact that the function describing the number of comments made on a given day is proportional to the total number of comments of the post. 
%In practice, this means that a few long, sustained interactions can drain attention just as effectively as many short ones.
We validated the model using empirical data from the subreddit \textit{Change My View}, where posts' creators engage with comments by rewarding them---an observable proxy for individual attention. 
Despite its simplicity, the model successfully reproduced both the decay in attention of the original post's creator and the interevent times between successive rewards. 
These findings underscore the model’s predictive power and its potential applicability to a wide range of online contexts, from email management to collaborative work platforms.

%By modeling how attention decays at the individual level, we provide a foundational tool for understanding user behavior in environments where engagement is constantly solicited. Our work complements and enriches the broader literature on collective attention by introducing a framework that can help platforms and designers better anticipate user overload, improve notification strategies, and foster healthier interaction patterns.

The model's simplicity trades off realism for analytical tractability, which opens several avenues for future research. 
For instance, one could incorporate heterogeneity in user fitness, linking it not only to activity but also to an intrinsic capacity to steer focal user's attention. 
In this setting, the individual attention dynamics is likely to depend also on the heterogeneity of the fitness distribution. 
Furthermore, one could validate our model on other data sets describing distinct social contexts, where the individual attention dynamics may be driven by different mechanisms.


%For example, one could incorporate heterogeneity in user preferences,
%cognitive capacities, or contextual relevance of stimuli could refine the predictions. 
%Additionally, extending the model to account for multitasking behavior or attention recovery after inactivity could yield deeper insights into digital attention management. As digital environments grow increasingly complex and demanding, understanding the limits of individual attention becomes not only scientifically interesting but also socially and ethically vital.


